Object.assign(window.FERC_DATA,{"reception":[{"group":"Data-center developers & hyperscalers","stance":"mixed","body":"Speed-to-power is the prize, but the trade is explicit: large customers are increasingly expected to bring credible capacity commitments, minimum charges, collateral, flexible operations or their own power strategy. Microsoft, Google, DCC, Equinix and operator voices all converge on the same point from different angles: the projects that look real are the ones that can show who pays, who curtails and where deliverable power comes from.","src":["amandapc","joshlevi","cymcgeady","arthurwm"]},{"group":"Transmission owners","stance":"mixed","body":"Named as respondents alongside the RTOs, TOs get clearer cost-allocation rules and a faster §206 lane than a generic rulemaking. The burden is real: they must help build the record, evaluate grid-enhancing technologies before defaulting to conventional upgrades, and answer whether existing tariffs can handle large-load risk without cross-subsidies.","src":["fercFS","bloomberglaw","jeffdennis"]},{"group":"Generators (gas / nuclear)","stance":"positive","body":"Dispatchable and firm-power suppliers benefit from the co-location and proximate-generation frame: a data center that can pair with deliverable generation is easier to study than a naked load request. That helps existing plants near load, but the clean-firm-power lane is also live; geothermal, storage, nuclear and grid-optimization procurement are part of the hyperscaler answer.","src":["utilitydive","timlatimer","luciatian","chaselochmiller"]},{"group":"State utility commissions","stance":"positive","body":"The bifurcation gives states wholesale-side transmission-cost visibility while preserving retail cost allocation and siting authority. That matters because the household-bill fight is already concrete: Maryland's ratepayer advocate and state lawmakers are challenging how PJM transmission costs tied to data-center load move across zones.","src":["fercFS","thehill","mdopc","bloomberglaw","naruc0618"]},{"group":"Consumer and environmental advocates","stance":"mixed","body":"The guardrail camp sees progress but not closure. Sierra Club called the announcement responsive on consumer-protection fronts; SELC wanted stronger federal standardization and clearer quarantine of data-center costs; Public Citizen remains more skeptical, tying fast interconnection to unresolved NERC reliability and state-authority concerns. State consumer advocates went further in July, asking FERC on rehearing to fix PJM's cost allocation itself rather than lean on state authority that, they argue, states do not clearly have.","src":["insideclimate","publiccitizen","benferc","bennerc","mdopc0717","udadvocates0721"]},{"group":"Grid-flexibility and demand-response providers","stance":"positive","body":"This camp gets a clearer market opening. Flexible-load operators argue that telemetry, controls and dispatchable demand can turn some data centers into grid assets and shorten study timelines. Their caveat is operational proof: the promise only works if the tariff turns flexibility into enforceable performance.","src":["varunsivaram","jeffbladen","norrisx0622"]},{"group":"Public power, co-ops and large customers","stance":"mixed","body":"Large customers and public-power interests mostly like faster interconnection when the beneficiary pays. Their worry is that vague cost causation turns into ordinary customers funding speculative upgrades, so the post-order debate keeps coming back to transparency, state coordination and bill-design mechanics.","src":["jeffdennis","mdopc"]},{"group":"Non-RTO Southeast and regional planners","stance":"negative","body":"The six orders cover organized markets, not the whole country. Southeast voices warn that manufacturers and data centers still need regional transmission planning outside the RTO/ISO frame, and that the regions with weak interconnection practice may be precisely where FERC's show-cause orders do not directly reach.","src":["utilitydive","simonmahan"]},{"group":"FERC (the Commission itself)","stance":"positive","body":"Unanimous action. Chairman Laura V. Swett: ‘We are setting the stage for a resilient, reliable, and forward-thinking grid that empowers communities and safeguards consumers… It also is critical that FERC provide certainty for investors by directing the markets to protect existing deals.’ The orders also invite (don’t foreclose) parallel § 205 filings.","src":["fercPR","fercFS"]}],"media":{"consensus":[{"t":"‘Historic’ / ‘landmark’: the framing is near-universal across trade press and the bar, with FERC asserting itself on load interconnection in an area it historically left alone.","src":["akin","rew","powermag"]},{"t":"Speed via procedure: § 206 show cause beats a multi-year NOPR; commentators read it as DOE’s April 30 deadline operationalized across all six markets at once.","src":["aaf","rtoinsider"]},{"t":"Regional flexibility reads as a deliberate design choice: SPP and PJM are treated as proactive leaders, others told to catch up without a uniform template.","src":["powermag","latitude"]},{"t":"Flexibility is becoming the price of speed: practitioners read curtailable / flexible service as the most valuable near-term lever to connect faster, turning a load’s willingness to limit usage into bargaining power.","src":["duanemorris","briggswhite","varunsivaram","jeffbladen","norrisx0622"]},{"t":"Built to survive appeal: legal analysts read the six tailored § 206 orders as deliberately litigation-resistant. A detailed, region-specific administrative record offers fewer openings to challenge than a single national rule, and it skips the multi-year lag of a rulemaking.","src":["bloomberglaw","heatmap","eenewsferc"]},{"t":"The public headline has escaped the energy-law lane: TechCrunch, AP, Tom's Hardware and Reddit all translate the orders as a data-center fast lane, even when they disagree on whether the real bottleneck is interconnection paperwork, physical power supply, or household bills.","src":["techcrunch","apdata","tomhardware","jigarferc","redditecon","reddittech"]},{"t":"Law-firm alerts read the orders as staying inside FERC's existing lines: no new or expanded jurisdiction over retail or co-located load, and a push for each region to propose its own §205 fix before FERC imposes one, with a warning that abeyances would not be granted reflexively.","src":["hklaw0623","hunton0626","baker0630"]},{"t":"X is treating the 60-day response clock as the live market story: energy/security analysts and infrastructure accounts are watching whether the RTO filings answer the real scarcity question, not just whether they satisfy FERC's process.","src":["xschifman","xnearco","xhalcyon"]}],"friction":[{"t":"Cost allocation is the central fight: how aggressively must large loads pre-fund and backstop upgrades, and over what crediting term? The ANOPR explicitly left crediting open.","src":["utilitydive","doe403"]},{"t":"Speed vs. rigor: the orders set a 45-day window to request abeyance of up to 90 days (NYISO P 42), which prevents indefinite stalling but compresses stakeholder process; the mandatory alternative-transmission-technology evaluation could itself add study time.","src":["e12","substack"]},{"t":"Coverage-gap questions: commenters flag that some transmission owners (e.g., Nebraska Public Power District, Great River Energy) appear absent from the published respondent lists, a completeness question to watch.","src":["substack"]},{"t":"The cost-allocation fight is already concrete and pending: Maryland’s ratepayer advocate, backed by 80 state lawmakers (June 17, 2026), says PJM’s tariff lets data-center transmission costs ‘leak’ across zones, ‘an unjust subsidy for that data center load’, and pegs Maryland’s exposure near $1.6 billion over a decade (FERC comment window extended to July 27, 2026).","src":["mdopc","utilitydive"]},{"t":"The state-authority premise is contested: Maryland's People's Counsel and advocates from Delaware, Illinois, Ohio and Pennsylvania argue the order leans on states to protect retail customers from costs that PJM's federally approved allocation sends them; states, they say, have no authority to move costs between transmission customers. Ari Peskoe backs them.","src":["mdopc0717","udadvocates0721","peskoe0720"]},{"t":"Definition gap: RMI notes that the large-load definition FERC suggests for the other RTOs (over 50 MW, taking service above 69 kV) does not reach facilities at or below 69 kV, which SPP's accepted definition does.","src":["rmi0706"]},{"t":"Fragmentation risk: six regional answers on six different timelines give multi-region developers clarity within a market but a patchwork across them, and fresh diligence for near-term deals.","src":["utilitydive","duanemorris"]},{"t":"The affordability backstop is split: FERC guards against cost-shifting among transmission customers but says preventing cost-shifting among retail customers is the states’ job, so the household-bill fight moves to state regulators even as the orders’ federal reach over load interconnection invites a jurisdictional challenge.","src":["thehill","bloomberglaw"]},{"t":"Fast lane vs. power supply: TechCrunch and Reddit frame the order as faster access to the grid, but AP and tech press stress the harder constraint: data-center construction is outrunning new generation, transmission upgrades and local consent.","src":["techcrunch","apdata","tomhardware","redditecon"]},{"t":"Reliability risk is now part of the same conversation: consumer advocates point to NERC's Level 3 data-center alert as the reason fast interconnection needs enforceable modeling, operating and ratepayer-protection standards.","src":["benferc","bennerc"]},{"t":"Developer reality check: Equinix's Arthur Haubenstock argues that data centers often cannot simply behave as flexible grid loads, and CoreWeave's Heather McGeory shows the scale has moved from megawatts to gigawatts. That complicates any policy story that treats curtailment or on-site supply as an easy fix.","src":["arthurwm","heathermcgeory"]},{"t":"The buildout is colliding with supply-chain physics: Briggs White points to 3- to 5-year gas-turbine lead times, 3- to 4-year transformer lead times and interconnection delays, so the FERC clock is only one part of a much larger delivery bottleneck.","src":["briggswhite"]},{"t":"Firm clean supply is the other half of the answer: Tim Latimer's Fervo work with Google and Lucia Tian's advanced-energy portfolio show why hyperscalers are pairing interconnection reform with geothermal, storage, nuclear and grid-optimization procurement, not only demand flexibility.","src":["timlatimer","luciatian"]},{"t":"The hyperscaler line is not monolithic but it is explicit on cost assignment: post-order coverage keeps pairing speed-to-power with large-customer cost responsibility, grid-utilization strategies, and organized federal advocacy from the data-center sector.","src":["amandapc","joshlevi","cymcneill","davidyoung"]},{"t":"AI competitiveness has become part of the power-grid argument: Equinix's Cy McGeady frames electricity supply as a bottleneck on U.S. AI dominance, while Tag Greason, Chase Lochmiller, Cully Cavness and Sara Axelrod show the operator side racing to deliver actual data-center capacity with energy attached.","src":["cymcgeady","taggreason","chaselochmiller","cullycavness","saraaxelrod"]},{"t":"Capital is watching energized gigawatts, not headline announcements: Shanu Mathew frames the investable question as which projects actually get power, interconnection and political permission, with FERC's reform aimed at slow and inconsistent rules.","src":["shanumathew"]},{"t":"The grid-connection problem has its own mechanics: Chris Gillett argues the country has enough electricity in aggregate, but the bottleneck is getting new loads and generation studied, connected and prioritized; Charles Hua adds that utility regulation and transparency shape whether consumers trust the answer.","src":["chrisgillett","charleshua"]},{"t":"The public narrative is settling around guardrails, not a simple yes/no on data centers: Jamie Nolan's Energy Empire framing is that data centers can either become the Loudoun County cautionary tale or a local-bill ally, depending on the deal structure.","src":["jamienolan"]},{"t":"The Southeast remains the outside-the-RTO pressure point: Simon Mahan argues that data centers and manufacturers need regional transmission planning, not just local patches, or the region risks higher prices and power shortages.","src":["simonmahan"]},{"t":"Congress may not leave the issue to FERC orders alone: the POWER Up Act would write a 100 MW large-load category into federal law and push standardized transmission-level interconnection rules, while saying state and local siting, generation and retail-rate authority remain intact.","src":["dcdpowerup"]},{"t":"After Oct 9, who pays is open again: one commentator says FERC got the law right and that new large loads should bring their own generation; another, that the largest grid still has no clear path for connecting data centers. A third reads FERC's jurisdictional reasoning as more nuanced than a flat rejection.","src":["enb1013","nyangon1013","konidena1012"]},{"t":"MISO's answer is splitting its own region: on the Oct 23 comment date for its LARS fast lane, Arkansas, Mississippi, Entergy, Cleco and MISO's transmission owners backed it, while clean-energy trade groups, consumer advocates and Google protested, and the states' committee asked for safeguards for projects already in the queue.","src":["udive1026","elibrary1030"]}],"outlets":["utilitydive","rtoinsider","latitude","tdworld","powermag","rew","akin","sheppard","natlaw","aaf","substack","heatmap","duanemorris","bloomberglaw","thehill","dcknowledge","enr","ieee","techcrunch","apdata","tomhardware","dcdpowerup","eenewsferc","xschifman","xnearco","xhalcyon","jigarferc","benferc","bennerc","arthurwm","jeffdennis","simonmahan","briggswhite","heathermcgeory","varunsivaram","timlatimer","jeffbladen","jamienolan","luciatian","saraaxelrod","chaselochmiller","cullycavness","amandapc","charleshua","chrisgillett","shanumathew","joshlevi","cymcgeady","cymcneill","davidyoung","taggreason","redditecon","reddittech","naruc0618","hklaw0623","hunton0626","baker0630","norrisx0622","rmi0706","mdopc0717","peskoe0720","udadvocates0721","wypr0826","dennisx1012","konidena1012","latitude1013","udduke1013","bisnow1014"]},"voices":[{"name":"CSIS Energy Security and Climate Change Program","affil":"Majkut, Garcia, Gilchrist and Jaffal","lean":"center","take":"Scored all six operators against the five reform categories three weeks after the orders and found the gap wide: of 30 matrix boxes, “only 6 are plausibly sufficient,” and “none of the six grid operators are likely to receive a clear pass through the five reforms.” Reads SPP as proof the targets are reachable inside the existing framework rather than evidence the bar is too high.","src":["csis"]},{"name":"Juan Arteaga","affil":"Enverus Intelligence Research","lean":"center","take":"Graded regional readiness two days before FERC granted the abeyances and put SPP alone at the front: “the only region with FERC-approved large-load tariffs.” The uncomfortable part is the mismatch he names, that “large-load development is concentrating in markets where key interconnection and cost-allocation rules are still evolving.”","src":["enverus"]},{"name":"Travis Kavulla","affil":"American Affairs · NRG","lean":"right","take":"Wants grid access priced, not rationed: borrow the natural-gas ‘open season’ so large loads bid for transferable interconnection rights instead of queuing first-come, first-served. Reads the orders’ cost-causation push as a step toward that, but short of market pricing.","src":["kavulla","kavullali"]},{"name":"Devin Hartman","affil":"R Street Institute","lean":"right","take":"Supportive of the design: calls the action ‘far more substantively ambitious than the ANOPR,’ with region-specific §206 pathways more effective than a uniform rule. Faults it mainly for leaving out non-RTO areas, which he says have the worst interconnection practices.","src":["utilitydive"]},{"name":"Sierra Club","affil":"environmental advocacy","lean":"left","take":"Cautiously positive: the announcement is ‘responsive to Sierra Club’s requests on several fronts, including protecting consumers from costs incurred by large loads,’ with the full orders still to be reviewed.","src":["insideclimate"]},{"name":"Southern Environmental Law Center","affil":"Nick Guidi, senior attorney","lean":"left","take":"Unconvinced by the cost guardrail: ‘There’s no real quarantining of the cost caused by data centers to those data centers,’ so it gets ‘spread throughout the whole customer base.’ Wanted more federal standardization than the orders deliver.","src":["insideclimate"]},{"name":"Public Citizen","affil":"consumer watchdog","lean":"left","take":"The most skeptical: pressed FERC for a temporary moratorium on new data-center interconnections until NERC reliability alerts are resolved, and warns against weakening state authority over data centers.","src":["publiccitizen"]},{"name":"Mona Dajani","affil":"Cooley LLP","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"Reads the six-order route as the end of any national template: ‘The era of one national standard for data center interconnection is over before it began.’ What replaces it, she says, is ‘six regional answers to the same question, decided on six different timelines’: clarity within each region, fragmentation across them.","src":["utilitydive"]},{"name":"Neil Chatterjee","affil":"former FERC chairman","lean":"right","take":"Calls it ‘a very FERC-y approach’ and says the design targets the core challenges ‘on a region by region basis on a tight timeline.’ He approves of the region-specific §206 route as faster and more legally defensible than one national rule.","src":["heatmap","eenewsferc"]},{"name":"Jennifer Danis","affil":"Institute for Policy Integrity","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"Reads the six-order route as built to survive appeal: a § 206 show cause yields ‘a much more robust and detailed administrative record’ tailored to each RTO, which she says ‘reduces future litigation entry points’ versus a single national rule.","src":["bloomberglaw"]},{"name":"Matthew Christiansen","affil":"Wilson Sonsini","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"On the federalism question: reads the orders as a legitimate exercise of FERC's transmission jurisdiction rather than an intrusion on the states. Says he doesn't think ‘anything that FERC proposed to do’ encroaches on state authority, so the state-versus-federal challenge critics expect starts from a narrow opening.","src":["bloomberglaw"]},{"name":"Jigar Shah","affil":"Deploy Action · former DOE Loan Programs Office","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"Reads the orders as a clever jurisdictional move: region-by-region § 206 orders avoid the federal-overreach fight while targeting the ‘speed to power’ bottleneck. His caveat is that FERC is not solving the price problem directly; data centers are an accelerant on a strained system, and the missing piece is a public capacity-and-demand picture.","src":["jigarferc"]},{"name":"Ben Inskeep","affil":"Citizens Action Coalition of Indiana","lean":"left","take":"Brings the consumer-advocate reliability lens: skeptical that data centers need a faster path to the grid, but encouraged that FERC is taking up large-load interconnection. His broader warning is that standards must protect ratepayers from negative stability, reliability and resiliency impacts as data centers proliferate.","src":["benferc","bennerc"]},{"name":"Aniruddh Mohan","affil":"The Brattle Group","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The post-order technical voice: flags the Maryland/PJM complaint as part of ‘increasing scrutiny on who pays’ for transmission to connect data centers, connecting the orders' cost-causation frame to a live regional fight.","src":["mohanx"]},{"name":"Arthur Haubenstock","affil":"Equinix","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The data-center-operator reality check: says the industry’s power needs collide with local grid impacts, backup-generation limits, and the real constraints behind ‘BYOP / BYONCE’ flexibility. Useful counterweight to the idea that every large load can simply curtail or bring its own supply.","src":["arthurwm"]},{"name":"Jeff Dennis","affil":"Electricity Customer Alliance · Coefficient Policy Experts","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The customer-affordability voice: supports FERC's move because it accelerates pathways for new large loads while reinforcing that those loads should bear the costs incurred to serve them, with more transparency and state coordination. In IEEE's coverage, he says FERC is threading the needle between speed-to-power and public concern over broader bill impacts. In October he read FERC's rejection of PJM's curtail-first service as putting more pressure on the states to find answers, and noted the order did not take up PJM's argument that the service matched SPP's approved CHILLS construct.","src":["jeffdennis","ieee","dennisx1012"]},{"name":"Ben Schifman","affil":"Institute for Progress","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The security-and-reliability skeptic: reads FERC's large-load action as meaningful interconnection reform, but flags that ‘security gaps remain’ as the grid absorbs more data-center demand.","src":["xschifman"]},{"name":"Simon Mahan","affil":"Southern Renewable Energy Association","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The Southeast planning voice: argues that manufacturers and data centers are bringing jobs to the region, but without a modern transmission system ‘we risk higher energy prices, power shortages, and lost economic opportunities.’ His point sharpens the non-RTO coverage gap the orders do not directly solve.","src":["simonmahan"]},{"name":"Briggs White","affil":"Keystone Ascent","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The buildout-and-supply-chain voice: sees hyperscaler demand as a physical infrastructure buildout, with co-location now assumed and emergency data-center load reduction ‘ripe for deeper industry analysis.’ His warning is that turbine, transformer and interconnection lead times force regulators to keep costs manageable while maintaining reliability.","src":["briggswhite"]},{"name":"Heather McGeory","affil":"CoreWeave","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"A hyperscaler power-procurement voice: says the conversation has moved from megawatts to gigawatts for individual data centers. Her presence in the discourse reinforces that AI infrastructure demand is no longer an abstract forecast; it is a siting, procurement and grid-modernization problem now.","src":["heathermcgeory"]},{"name":"Varun Sivaram","affil":"Emerald AI","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The software-flexibility voice: argues data centers can become grid assets, not just fixed loads, by dynamically adjusting demand to relieve grid stress while maintaining performance.","src":["varunsivaram"]},{"name":"Tim Latimer","affil":"Fervo Energy","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The firm-clean-power voice: Fervo's Google data-center deals show the supply-side counterpart to FERC's load-interconnection push: hyperscalers are also trying to procure 24/7 geothermal power near the places where AI load is growing.","src":["timlatimer"]},{"name":"Jeff Bladen","affil":"Verrus","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The asset-backed-flexibility operator: argues a flexible data center can be a grid asset, not only a consumer, and that physical storage, controls and telemetry make that different from old demand-response programs.","src":["jeffbladen"]},{"name":"Jamie Nolan","affil":"Energy Empire · Nolan Strategic Communications","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The public-narrative voice: frames data centers as a guardrails problem, not a binary good-or-bad story. Done wrong, they are the Loudoun County cautionary tale; done right, she argues they can become a local-bill ally rather than the villain driving bills up.","src":["jamienolan"]},{"name":"Lucia Tian","affil":"Google","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The Google advanced-energy voice: frames AI's power demand as a race to secure carbon-free power through geothermal, nuclear, long-duration storage and grid optimization. That makes the hyperscaler story about procuring new clean supply, not only asking grid operators for faster interconnection.","src":["luciatian"]},{"name":"Sara Axelrod","affil":"Crusoe","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The Crusoe policy voice: says AI compute is making energy the defining constraint and competitive advantage for data infrastructure, and frames Crusoe's answer as energy-first infrastructure that co-locates compute with abundant or stranded power.","src":["saraaxelrod"]},{"name":"Chase Lochmiller","affil":"Crusoe","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The AI-campus builder voice: says developers are pairing data-center growth with behind-the-meter generation and siting near stranded or underused power. His Stargate work makes the large-load debate concrete: build compute where power can actually be delivered.","src":["chaselochmiller"]},{"name":"Cully Cavness","affil":"Crusoe","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The bridge-to-grid voice: describes gas as a bridge to grid power at Abilene / Stargate, showing how AI data centers are turning into power-development projects before the grid can catch up.","src":["cullycavness"]},{"name":"Amanda Peterson Corio","affil":"Google","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The Google grid-utilization voice: says the sector has to find solutions that better utilize the system. Google works on data-center flexibility, but she also argues it can be faster and more cost effective to pay other customers to shift load because idle chips are expensive.","src":["amandapc"]},{"name":"Charles Hua","affil":"PowerLines","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The utility-regulation voice: calls data centers arguably the most important topic in energy, but warns that blaming every bill increase on data centers is too simple. His focus is utility incentives, transparency and whether regulators center consumers as the grid is rebuilt.","src":["charleshua"]},{"name":"Chris Gillett","affil":"Works in Progress","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The interconnection-mechanics voice: argues America has enough electricity in aggregate; the hard part is getting new data centers and power plants studied and connected to the grid, and flexible connection rights could let some loads move faster.","src":["chrisgillett"]},{"name":"Shanu Mathew","affil":"Energy, power and infrastructure investor","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The capital-markets voice: focuses on energized GW rather than announced demand, with power constraints and political resistance as the variables that decide which data-center projects become real. On FERC, he reads the reform as fixing slow, inconsistent interconnection rules and making full upgrade-cost responsibility part of the bargain.","src":["shanumathew"]},{"name":"Josh Levi","affil":"Data Center Coalition","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The data-center trade-association voice: pushes back on data centers being singled out for grid impacts and argues the sector needs utilities, grid operators and policymakers to grow infrastructure and recognize data centers as part of the modern economy.","src":["joshlevi"]},{"name":"Cy McGeady","affil":"Equinix · former CSIS","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The Equinix energy-policy voice: argues electricity supply is a bottleneck for U.S. AI dominance, with data-center investment moving toward regions where generation, permitting and land-use conditions allow rapid buildout.","src":["cymcgeady"]},{"name":"Cy McNeill","affil":"Data Center Coalition","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The DCC federal-affairs voice: works the transmission, siting, permitting and energy-market advocacy lane for the data-center industry. Distinct from Cy McGeady at Equinix, McNeill represents the association's federal policy posture.","src":["cymcneill"]},{"name":"David Young","affil":"Equinix · Data Center Coalition","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The data-center federal-policy voice: represents the Equinix/DCC lane in the coalition's Federal Policy Leadership Advisory Council, reinforcing that the industry is organizing around federal power, permitting and grid-access questions.","src":["davidyoung"]},{"name":"Tag Greason","affil":"QTS Data Centers","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The data-center capacity-delivery voice: from the operator side, emphasizes hyperscale AI demand and the bottlenecks that determine whether capacity can actually be delivered, not just announced.","src":["taggreason"]},{"name":"Larry Gasteiger","affil":"WIRES (transmission trade group)","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"From the transmission-builder camp: reads the show cause route as the faster path, since ‘going through the show cause process makes a lot of sense, because you don't have all of the time lags associated with a lengthy rulemaking process.’","src":["bloomberglaw"]},{"name":"Ari Peskoe","affil":"Harvard Law School Electricity Law Initiative","lean":"center","take":"The legal critic from the consumer side. Backs the four-state advocates' rehearing argument against the PJM order: ‘That move violates the law. PJM’s Tariff guarantees cost shifts due to transmission for data centers. Only FERC can fix that.’ Days later he added that FERC ordered no change to how utilities share upgrade costs, so those costs are still spread to everyone, the case his April op-ed made against FERC's transmission-pricing policy. In September he called FERC's objection to the backstop's cost allocation surprising.","src":["peskoe0720","peskoe0723","peskoe0930","peskoe"]},{"name":"David S. Lapp","affil":"Maryland People’s Counsel","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The state consumer advocate's case against the PJM order. His office's July 17 filing, joined by the Ohio, Delaware and Illinois advocates, argues the order exposes data-center cost shifts without fixing the PJM rules that cause them, and that it ‘erroneously assumes State regulators have authority that they do not clearly have.’ On PJM's backstop proposal he warned that if forecast data centers never arrive, existing customers, households included, pay the cost.","src":["mdopc0717","udadvocates0721","wypr0826"]},{"name":"National Association of Regulatory Utility Commissioners","affil":"Ann Rendahl, NARUC President (Washington UTC)","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The state regulators' first word, issued the day of the orders. Reserved judgment until the orders were posted, welcomed the commissioners' ‘recognizing the critical role of states and state regulators,’ and said states remain committed to allocating costs appropriately across customer classes.","src":["naruc0618"]},{"name":"RMI","affil":"Sarah Wang and Tyler Farrell","lean":"center","take":"The definition-gap read. FERC accepted SPP's tiered definition (over 10 MW at 69 kV or below, over 50 MW above it), while ‘FERC’s suggested definition for other RTOs does not address facilities interconnecting at or below 69 kV,’ which leaves each operator to decide whether to write its own.","src":["rmi0706"]},{"name":"Claire Lang-Ree","affil":"NRDC Sustainable FERC Project","lean":"left","take":"The environmental-advocate case for making data centers bring their own power. In August she welcomed PJM's backstop proposal as PJM declining to keep buying power on data centers' behalf. After FERC rejected PJM's curtail-first service on Oct 9, NRDC criticized the ruling and she urged PJM to refile: ‘At a minimum, PJM’s rules must require data centers to bring their own supply, and not allow them to raise electricity costs or cause blackouts.’","src":["bisnow1014","wypr0826"]},{"name":"Tyler Norris","affil":"Duke University Nicholas Institute","lean":"center","take":"The researcher behind the flexible-load headroom estimate the flexible-service reform leans on. Four days after the orders he thanked FERC for its bipartisan leadership, ‘especially on establishing new flexible service options for large loads.’","src":["norrisx0622","norris"]},{"name":"Chris Wright","affil":"U.S. Secretary of Energy","lean":"right","take":"The directing agency, declaring victory: DOE applauds the orders as speed-to-power that still protects consumers. Secretary Wright frames them as the Administration working to ‘remove barriers, accelerate development’ and to deliver on ‘President Trump's Ratepayer Protection Pledge.’ In October DOE backed FERC's backstop order for PJM, with Wright saying large electricity users should pay for the new generation built to serve them.","src":["doeApplaud","doe1009"]},{"name":"Elliot Mainzer","affil":"California ISO (President and CEO)","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"Frames CAISO's Large Loads proposal, advanced by its Board on Oct 28 for a filing by Nov 16, as the show cause answer itself: ‘Our response to the FERC order to show cause will improve planning and operational awareness of new large loads on the system, prevent cost-shifting, encourage flexibility and expedite large load energization.’ Pitches it as staying ahead of large-load growth without ‘placing an undue burden on consumers.’","src":["appa1028"]},{"name":"Organization of MISO States","affil":"regional state committee for MISO","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The states' check on MISO's fast lane for large-load generation. In comments on LARS (ER27-25), as Utility Dive quoted them, it draws the line at a bypass: ‘LARS cannot become a general alternative to the existing [generation interconnection queue] or provide preferential access to transmission capacity without adequate safeguards for existing queue participants and transmission customers.’","src":["udive1026"]},{"name":"Corporate Energy Buyers Association and Data Center Coalition","affil":"large energy buyers and data-center operators (joint letter)","lean":"nonpartisan","take":"The customers the show cause order is meant to serve, asking PJM not to answer it with a queue overhaul. Their Oct 2 letter to PJM's Board: ‘PJM has not demonstrated that this proposal will deliver the speed, certainty, or transparency envisioned by the Show Cause Order.’ They want either a direct answer to the 60-to-90-day study directive or a narrower filing now.","src":["cebadcc1002"]},{"name":"Chris Van Hollen","affil":"U.S. Senator (D-Md.)","lean":"left","take":"Puts FERC alongside the White House as failing ratepayers on data centers: ‘Clearly, this admin & FERC are not protecting consumers.’ Pitches his Power for the People Act as the fix (X, Oct 21).","src":["vanhollenx1021"]}],"voiceThemes":[{"wave":1,"title":"Cost responsibility is the center of gravity","body":"Across the post-order discussion, the basic question is not whether large loads should connect faster; it is whether the tariff makes the beneficiary carry the cost and risk of the upgrade.","quotes":[{"q":"There’s no real quarantining of the cost caused by data centers to those data centers","src":"insideclimate"},{"q":"those loads bear the costs incurred to serve them","src":"jeffdennis"},{"q":"increasing scrutiny on who pays for transmission to connect data centers","src":"mohanx"},{"q":"President Trump's Ratepayer Protection Pledge","src":"doeApplaud"}]},{"wave":1,"title":"The regional route is both substance and litigation strategy","body":"The six-order structure is being read as a deliberate alternative to one national template: faster than a rulemaking, more tailored to each market, and easier to defend on the record.","quotes":[{"q":"far more substantively ambitious than the ANOPR","src":"utilitydive"},{"q":"six regional answers to the same question, decided on six different timelines","src":"utilitydive"},{"q":"a much more robust and detailed administrative record","src":"bloomberglaw"},{"q":"reduces future litigation entry points","src":"bloomberglaw"}]},{"wave":1,"title":"Speed now depends on provable flexibility","body":"The strongest pro-speed argument treats data centers as controllable loads, but the quotes also show the hard edge: flexibility has to be operational, measurable, and worth something in the tariff.","quotes":[{"q":"emergency data-center load reduction ripe for deeper industry analysis","src":"briggswhite"},{"q":"flexible AI data centers can adjust demand dynamically and act as grid assets, not only as fixed loads","src":"varunsivaram"},{"q":"asset-backed flexible data centers can operate as grid assets","src":"jeffbladen"},{"q":"paying other customers to shift load can be faster and more cost effective than curtailing expensive data-center chips","src":"amandapc"}]},{"wave":1,"title":"Reliability and security remain live objections","body":"The order is not landing as a pure acceleration story. Consumer and security voices are treating faster interconnection as acceptable only if modeling, operating standards, and stability protections keep up.","quotes":[{"q":"security gaps remain","src":"xschifman"},{"q":"data centers need any more help with interconnection to the grid","src":"benferc"},{"q":"standards and rules in place that protect ratepayers from any negative stability, reliability and resiliency impacts","src":"benferc"}]},{"wave":1,"title":"The physical buildout is bigger than the FERC clock","body":"The discourse keeps returning to the same physical constraint: the orders can compress process, but turbines, transformers, generation, local consent, and deliverable capacity still decide which projects energize.","quotes":[{"q":"power conversations for individual data centers have moved from megawatts to gigawatts","src":"heathermcgeory"},{"q":"gas-turbine, transformer and interconnection lead times","src":"briggswhite"},{"q":"power constraints and political resistance","src":"shanumathew"},{"q":"developers are pairing data-center growth with behind-the-meter generation and siting near stranded or underused power","src":"chaselochmiller"}]},{"wave":1,"title":"The organized-market focus leaves a Southeast gap","body":"The order covers the six RTO/ISO markets, while several reactions flag the regions outside that structure as exactly where transmission planning and interconnection practice may be weakest.","quotes":[{"q":"non-RTO areas, which typically suffer from the worst transmission and interconnection practices","src":"utilitydive"},{"q":"The Southeast needs a modern transmission system","src":"simonmahan"},{"q":"we risk higher energy prices, power shortages, and lost economic opportunities","src":"simonmahan"}]},{"wave":1,"captured":"2026-10-20","title":"The state line: FERC claims no new retail reach","body":"On the day of the orders the state regulators' association reserved judgment on substance but welcomed the commissioners' nod to state authority. A law-firm read of the orders put the same point plainly: FERC stayed inside its existing jurisdiction and left retail questions with the states. By July, state consumer advocates were arguing that this was the problem; their case is in the summer lane.","quotes":[{"q":"We appreciate the Commissioners’ comments at today’s meeting recognizing the critical role of states and state regulators given the jurisdictional division of duties under the Federal Power Act.","src":"naruc0618"},{"q":"States remain committed to ensuring the appropriate allocation of costs across all customer classes and working constructively with our federal colleagues to that end.","src":"naruc0618"},{"q":"Importantly, FERC did not assert any new or expanded jurisdiction over retail load or co-located load in a broad or preemptive sense.","src":"hklaw0623"}]},{"wave":2,"track":"gov","title":"PJM’s own legitimacy becomes the story","body":"The July 23 technical conference put PJM’s decision-making machinery on the record, and on a September clock. The same commission running the §206 filings is now asking who gets to decide what PJM files, which makes this lane a precondition for the other one rather than a sideshow.","quotes":[{"q":"PJM is facing a grave legitimacy crisis","src":"udgov"},{"q":"Some transmission owners are openly discussing leaving the RTO altogether","src":"udgov"},{"q":"This is a cultural quagmire","src":"udgov"}]},{"wave":2,"track":"sc6","title":"Regional divergence: the tailoring thesis meets the record","body":"The first observed answer to the 30-day reports points somewhere different from PJM’s expedited-new-entry posture. ISO-NE looks to new generation and away from its capacity market. That is the regional variation the six tailored orders were built to permit, showing up as substance rather than as a gap.","quotes":[{"q":"requiring new large loads to bring incremental new generation","src":"isonews0721"},{"q":"not procuring capacity through the regional capacity market to serve these large loads","src":"isonews0721"},{"q":"intend to request a 90-day abeyance","src":"isonews0629"}]},{"wave":2,"track":"context","title":"Capacity scarcity is the arithmetic behind the argument","body":"PJM’s 2028/2029 auction cleared at its cap for the third year running and still landed short of the reserve-margin target. Every claim about who should pay for new load is being made against that number.","quotes":[{"q":"The price came in at the FERC-approved cap of $325/MW-day","src":"pjmbra"},{"q":"short of PJM’s 20% installed reserve margin target by 6,831 MW","src":"pjmbra"}]},{"wave":2,"track":"sc6","captured":"2026-10-20","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","body":"On July 17 Maryland's People's Counsel, joined by the Ohio, Delaware and Illinois advocates, asked FERC to rehear the PJM order, and Pennsylvania's advocate filed separately. Their argument is that the order makes data-center transmission costs visible to states but leaves PJM's cost allocation in place, and states cannot move costs between transmission customers. Ari Peskoe of Harvard's Electricity Law Initiative sided with them. FERC dismissed every request on Sept 21 because the order had not decided anything yet.","quotes":[{"q":"The order erroneously assumes State regulators have authority that they do not clearly have.","src":"mdopc0717"},{"q":"Each network upgrade added to a transmission owner’s revenue requirement while these questions remain open embeds another cost shift","src":"udadvocates0721"},{"q":"Unjust and unreasonable wholesale transmission cost shifts from large loads in PJM can manifest through unjust and unreasonable cost allocation of both [Regional Transmission Expansion Plan] projects and supplemental projects","src":"udadvocates0721"},{"q":"That move violates the law. PJM's Tariff guarantees cost shifts due to transmission for data centers. Only FERC can fix that. And it must.","src":"peskoe0720"},{"q":"It did not order the utilities to charge those upgrade costs to data centers. They are still spread to everyone.","src":"peskoe0723"},{"q":"We therefore dismiss the requests for rehearing, which concern a Commission action that is not final and that will be succeeded by further Commission action.","src":"rehearing0921"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"FERC draws the retail line under PJM's curtail-first service","body":"The Oct 9 order on ER26-3515 is the first FERC ruling on large-load tariff text since June 18, and the ground it chose was jurisdiction rather than policy. The interim service fell because it reached retail customers; the definition and the registry survived because they do not. Analysts read the result as support for existing generators, and PJM's own reaction kept to cost causation.","quotes":[{"q":"several provisions of the proposed Tariff appear to place obligations directly on Eligible New Large Loads, which are, for the most part, retail customers","src":"llorder1009"},{"q":"the proposed compensation framework appears to establish a retail rate","src":"llorder1009"},{"q":"we strongly encourage states to continue their important work on this issue","src":"llorder1009"},{"q":"honoring the concept that existing electricity customers should not be unfairly burdened by new, energy intensive customers","src":"udive1012"},{"q":"the latest development is positive for existing generation/IPPs","src":"udive1012"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"Rosner: curtail data centers last, and only after demand response","body":"The one separate statement on the Oct 9 order. Commissioner Rosner agreed with rejecting the package and wrote to say what a refiled version would have to justify: forcing new large loads off the system before customers who volunteered for demand response, and excluding those loads from the capacity-market demand curve while the region sheds them involuntarily.","quotes":[{"q":"That’s not only economically inefficient, but also likely to prevent supply and demand from coming back into balance.","src":"llorder1009"},{"q":"We need state regulators to establish clear rules for which customers should be curtailed first when circumstances require it.","src":"llorder1009"},{"q":"such measures should only be taken when necessary to keep the system reliable, after voluntary demand response resources have been exhausted","src":"llorder1009"},{"q":"in an emergency, data centers not serving critical functions should be shut off before ordinary customers","src":"llorder1009"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"PJM answers two setbacks with refilings, not rehearing","body":"After the Sept 29 backstop order and the Oct 9 large-load order, PJM's public posture is the same both times: review, then refile fast. Its Oct 22 consultation deck names Oct 29 for the new backstop filing and lists the fixes, including dropping the municipal and cooperative opt-out FERC said may be unduly discriminatory.","quotes":[{"q":"PJM is reviewing the order and intends to work quickly to address the Commission’s remaining concerns.","src":"pjmil0930"},{"q":"PJM submitted a motion for abeyance of the paper hearing on October 12 and notified FERC that it plans to submit a new 205 filing to FERC on October 29th","src":"pjmmc1022"},{"q":"Remove the opt out for muni/co-ops.","src":"pjmmc1022"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"sc6","title":"Nov 16 is turning into a §205 date","body":"Four weeks before the reset deadline, the respondents are saying in their own documents that they will file tariff changes rather than defend the status quo: the PJM transmission owners and ISO-NE in writing on Oct 9, MISO by filing pieces as it goes, PJM with a ride-through standard it says it will file in November.","quotes":[{"q":"The PJM Transmission Owners have committed to submit their proposed revisions to address the concerns identified by the June 18, 2026 order issued in Docket No. EL26-67-000","src":"pjmto1009"},{"q":"The ISO and the Participating Transmission Owners Administrative Committee (PTO AC) were granted an abeyance and will develop a Section 205 filing to be submitted by November 16, 2026.","src":"isone1009"},{"q":"LARS provides MISO with a targeted tool to address the intersection of significant large load additions,","src":"udive1005"},{"q":"This type of operation is unsustainable and needs to be addressed,","src":"pjmil0910"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","body":"PJM's backstop buys capacity for a shortfall that large loads are driving, so the argument is over who carries the cost. Before FERC ruled, NRDC's Claire Lang-Ree read the proposal as PJM refusing to keep buying power for data centers, and Maryland People's Counsel David Lapp warned that households would pay if the forecast load never arrived. Two concurrences on the Sept 29 order answered: Chairman Swett faulted a filing made on the last possible day, and Commissioner See wrote that existing customers should not carry costs new demand causes. Ari Peskoe called FERC's cost-allocation objection surprising; DOE backed the order on Oct 9.","quotes":[{"q":"PJM is saying very clearly to the data centers, 'We will no longer buy power on your behalf,'","src":"wypr0826"},{"q":"If the data centers don't show up in those zones, then it'll be the existing customers, ordinary households included, that will pay those costs.","src":"wypr0826"},{"q":"This Commission will not be forced into accepting a deeply flawed, eleventh-hour procurement mechanism with billion-dollar implications for consumers.","src":"backstop0929"},{"q":"Existing customers should not be left paying costs attributable to new demand.","src":"backstop0929"},{"q":"Surprising! FERC has issues with the cost allocation framework that determined who pays for this data center capacity auction.","src":"peskoe0930"},{"q":"the Energy Department supports having large electricity users pay for reliable new power generation","src":"doe1009"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"After Oct 9, the states hold the pen on curtailment","body":"FERC rejected PJM's curtail-first service as a reach into retail jurisdiction, and the reactions agree on the consequence: the next move belongs to the states. Jeff Dennis read the order as more pressure on them. Mary Yang, a former PJM regulatory counsel now at 804 Advisory, said it slows bring-your-own-capacity deals. NRDC wants PJM to refile with a bring-your-own-supply rule. And in a Duke Energy Carolinas order the same day, outside the RTOs, Commissioner Rosner wrote that FERC may have to widen the show cause orders' cost-recovery framework if state action falls short.","quotes":[{"q":"In doing so, it appears to be putting more pressure on states to find answers.","src":"dennisx1012"},{"q":"It does slow down the development of these more innovative bilateral mechanisms to solve some of these problems","src":"latitude1013"},{"q":"At a minimum, PJM's rules must require data centers to bring their own supply, and not allow them to raise electricity costs or cause blackouts","src":"bisnow1014"},{"q":"To the extent state regulatory actions are not sufficient to protect consumers, the Commission has an obligation to seriously consider expanding the cost recovery framework it established in the Large Load Show Cause Orders","src":"dukeorder1009"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"The backstop comes back, and the rehearing requests hedge","body":"PJM refiled the backstop on the last day FERC's Sept 29 order allowed, as a new §205 filing rather than a brief in the paper hearing, and says it has answered all three issues FERC set for hearing. The same day, seven parties had requests for rehearing or clarification of the Sept 29 order on file. Consumer advocates from five jurisdictions want the market-power and price-cap terms fixed in whatever PJM files next; the Indicated PJM Transmission Owners said outright that they hope the refiling makes their request moot.","quotes":[{"q":"PJM filed an amended proposal to FERC with the goal of commencing a Reliability Backstop Procurement on March 1, 2027.","src":"pjmil1029"},{"q":"Through this section 205 filing, PJM has addressed the issues set for paper hearing in the September 29 Order.","src":"pjmrbp1029"},{"q":"permits the unmitigated exercise of seller-side market power","src":"jcareh1029"},{"q":"submit a revised section 205 filing that supersedes its earlier section 205 filing and may moot the need for rehearing of the Order","src":"toreh1029"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"sc6","title":"CAISO's board signs off, and PJM books its consultation","body":"Two of the six respondents moved on Nov 16 in the last days of October. CAISO's Board advanced the Large Loads proposal and its chief executive described the filing as the show cause response. PJM skipped the Oct 28 advisory vote the abeyance order had recorded and put a Nov 5 consultation on the calendar for several §205 filings rather than one.","quotes":[{"q":"Our response to the FERC order to show cause will improve planning and operational awareness of new large loads on the system, prevent cost-shifting, encourage flexibility and expedite large load energization","src":"appa1028"},{"q":"We look forward to our ongoing collaboration with our participating transmission owners, state energy agencies and industry stakeholders to make sure we stay ahead of the curve on interconnecting large loads without compromising reliability or placing an undue burden on consumers","src":"appa1028"},{"q":"PJM staff will consult with stakeholders on plans to submit a suite of section 205 filings to address Tariff revisions to address opportunities identified in the Large Load Show Cause Order and related reforms.","src":"pjmmc1105"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"MISO's fast lane for large loads splits its own footprint","body":"MISO's Large Load Addition Resource Study would let new generation for large loads move ahead of the ordinary queue. On the Oct 23 comment date the states' committee asked for safeguards for everyone already waiting, Google said the design lacks protection against shifting costs to other customers, and the Data Center Coalition, while generally supportive, objected that utilities would decide which projects get in. The quotes are from the filings as Utility Dive printed them.","quotes":[{"q":"LARS cannot become a general alternative to the existing [generation interconnection queue] or provide preferential access to transmission capacity without adequate safeguards for existing queue participants and transmission customers","src":"udive1026"},{"q":"The lack of cost shifting protections is especially concerning because, despite the benefits — especially speed to power — that LARS would promote, it is structured in a way that threatens Google’s ability to meet its foundational commitment to ensuring that other retail customers do not bear the costs of serving its data centers, including through the Ratepayer Protection Pledge","src":"udive1026"},{"q":"In MISO’s primarily vertically integrated footprint, establishing the LSE as the sole LARS application facilitator narrows the pool of supply that can gain access to expedited Large Load Generation Interconnection Agreements ... to only those projects that are effectively approved by the LSE","src":"udive1026"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"sc6","title":"The customers ask PJM not to answer with a queue overhaul","body":"The show cause order exists to get large loads connected faster, and the large loads are now arguing PJM's answer would slow them down. PJM told FERC in October that it will file a unified study of large loads and generation by Nov 16. The energy buyers and data-center operators who would use it wrote to PJM's Board on Oct 2 that a combined cluster with an annual cutoff could mean nearly two years between transmission owner approval and the end of PJM's study, and that MISO and SPP generally run load and generation planning in parallel instead.","quotes":[{"q":"While the Order calls for improvements to large load integration, it does not require PJM to overhaul its entire generation interconnection queue.","src":"cebadcc1002"},{"q":"PJM has not demonstrated that this proposal will deliver the speed, certainty, or transparency envisioned by the Show Cause Order.","src":"cebadcc1002"}]},{"wave":3,"track":"context","title":"Bring your own power, as a presidential line","body":"The idea that large loads should supply their own electricity has run through this record since June, from consumer advocates to PJM's backstop. On Oct 21 the President stated it as a mandate, with no docket or instrument attached, and the same day a Maryland senator, in a post of his own, put FERC alongside the White House as the problem.","quotes":[{"q":"New and large, in some cases gigantic, Data Centers are mandated by me to make their own electricity, and give or sell any excess back to the local community, or grid","src":"bisnow1021"},{"q":"When push comes to shove, Trump would rather you lose power than the data center companies lose a cent of profit.","src":"vanhollenx1021"}]}],"discourse":[{"id":"quote-d0ff1a62d4","kind":"quote","text":"There’s no real quarantining of the cost caused by data centers to those data centers","title":"Cost responsibility is the center of gravity","src":["insideclimate"],"date":"2026-06-23","wave":1,"themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-2908d1e432","kind":"quote","text":"those loads bear the costs incurred to serve them","title":"Cost responsibility is the center of gravity","src":["jeffdennis"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-7df729d925","kind":"quote","text":"increasing scrutiny on who pays for transmission to connect data centers","title":"Cost responsibility is the center of gravity","src":["mohanx"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-5117c23a20","kind":"quote","text":"President Trump's Ratepayer Protection Pledge","title":"Cost responsibility is the center of gravity","src":["doeApplaud"],"date":"2026-06-28","wave":1,"themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-7ffd2576e5","kind":"quote","text":"far more substantively ambitious than the ANOPR","title":"The regional route is both substance and litigation strategy","src":["utilitydive"],"date":"2026-06-22","wave":1,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-8341c81fdf","kind":"quote","text":"six regional answers to the same question, decided on six different timelines","title":"The regional route is both substance and litigation strategy","src":["utilitydive"],"date":"2026-06-22","wave":1,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-6134d512e4","kind":"quote","text":"a much more robust and detailed administrative record","title":"The regional route is both substance and litigation strategy","src":["bloomberglaw"],"date":"2026-06-27","wave":1,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-a941dafed7","kind":"quote","text":"reduces future litigation entry points","title":"The regional route is both substance and litigation strategy","src":["bloomberglaw"],"date":"2026-06-27","wave":1,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-ee35cc9d91","kind":"quote","text":"emergency data-center load reduction ripe for deeper industry analysis","title":"Speed now depends on provable flexibility","src":["briggswhite"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-92ee47349b","kind":"quote","text":"flexible AI data centers can adjust demand dynamically and act as grid assets, not only as fixed loads","title":"Speed now depends on provable flexibility","src":["varunsivaram"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-c0dc86d8b9","kind":"quote","text":"asset-backed flexible data centers can operate as grid assets","title":"Speed now depends on provable flexibility","src":["jeffbladen"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-f8454c7336","kind":"quote","text":"paying other customers to shift load can be faster and more cost effective than curtailing expensive data-center chips","title":"Speed now depends on provable flexibility","src":["amandapc"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["cost","flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-864ccc9cf2","kind":"quote","text":"security gaps remain","title":"Reliability and security remain live objections","src":["xschifman"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-a6bc65332c","kind":"quote","text":"data centers need any more help with interconnection to the grid","title":"Reliability and security remain live objections","src":["benferc"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-8e802a80d1","kind":"quote","text":"standards and rules in place that protect ratepayers from any negative stability, reliability and resiliency impacts","title":"Reliability and security remain live objections","src":["benferc"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-09f0c68bd3","kind":"quote","text":"power conversations for individual data centers have moved from megawatts to gigawatts","title":"The physical buildout is bigger than the FERC clock","src":["heathermcgeory"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-875495e814","kind":"quote","text":"gas-turbine, transformer and interconnection lead times","title":"The physical buildout is bigger than the FERC clock","src":["briggswhite"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-bd3a6ef840","kind":"quote","text":"power constraints and political resistance","title":"The physical buildout is bigger than the FERC clock","src":["shanumathew"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-fb74b7a121","kind":"quote","text":"developers are pairing data-center growth with behind-the-meter generation and siting near stranded or underused power","title":"The physical buildout is bigger than the FERC clock","src":["chaselochmiller"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":["colo"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-3fe95fc066","kind":"quote","text":"non-RTO areas, which typically suffer from the worst transmission and interconnection practices","title":"The organized-market focus leaves a Southeast gap","src":["utilitydive"],"date":"2026-06-22","wave":1,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-384a7d02ef","kind":"quote","text":"The Southeast needs a modern transmission system","title":"The organized-market focus leaves a Southeast gap","src":["simonmahan"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-917f447602","kind":"quote","text":"we risk higher energy prices, power shortages, and lost economic opportunities","title":"The organized-market focus leaves a Southeast gap","src":["simonmahan"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":1,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-36c3cae9ee","kind":"quote","text":"We appreciate the Commissioners’ comments at today’s meeting recognizing the critical role of states and state regulators given the jurisdictional division of duties under the Federal Power Act.","title":"The state line: FERC claims no new retail reach","src":["naruc0618"],"date":"2026-06-18","wave":1,"themes":["retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-ad9b5332da","kind":"quote","text":"States remain committed to ensuring the appropriate allocation of costs across all customer classes and working constructively with our federal colleagues to that end.","title":"The state line: FERC claims no new retail reach","src":["naruc0618"],"date":"2026-06-18","wave":1,"themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-47c79e0362","kind":"quote","text":"Importantly, FERC did not assert any new or expanded jurisdiction over retail load or co-located load in a broad or preemptive sense.","title":"The state line: FERC claims no new retail reach","src":["hklaw0623"],"date":"2026-06-23","wave":1,"themes":["colo","retail"],"groups":["pjm-colocation"]},{"id":"quote-ce3bc247ec","kind":"quote","text":"PJM is facing a grave legitimacy crisis","title":"PJM’s own legitimacy becomes the story","src":["udgov"],"date":"2026-07-24","wave":2,"themes":[],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-9625512c32","kind":"quote","text":"Some transmission owners are openly discussing leaving the RTO altogether","title":"PJM’s own legitimacy becomes the story","src":["udgov"],"date":"2026-07-24","wave":2,"themes":[],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-1b7c5d9283","kind":"quote","text":"This is a cultural quagmire","title":"PJM’s own legitimacy becomes the story","src":["udgov"],"date":"2026-07-24","wave":2,"themes":[],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-ebb6d59459","kind":"quote","text":"requiring new large loads to bring incremental new generation","title":"Regional divergence: the tailoring thesis meets the record","src":["isonews0721"],"date":"2026-07-21","wave":2,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-8a12df4ae9","kind":"quote","text":"not procuring capacity through the regional capacity market to serve these large loads","title":"Regional divergence: the tailoring thesis meets the record","src":["isonews0721"],"date":"2026-07-21","wave":2,"themes":["adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-1e24a94798","kind":"quote","text":"intend to request a 90-day abeyance","title":"Regional divergence: the tailoring thesis meets the record","src":["isonews0629"],"date":"2026-06-29","wave":2,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-b35bacbe59","kind":"quote","text":"The price came in at the FERC-approved cap of $325/MW-day","title":"Capacity scarcity is the arithmetic behind the argument","src":["pjmbra"],"date":"2026-07-14","wave":2,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-cff99b12ca","kind":"quote","text":"short of PJM’s 20% installed reserve margin target by 6,831 MW","title":"Capacity scarcity is the arithmetic behind the argument","src":["pjmbra"],"date":"2026-07-14","wave":2,"themes":[],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-ab1a568862","kind":"quote","text":"The order erroneously assumes State regulators have authority that they do not clearly have.","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","src":["mdopc0717"],"date":"2026-07-17","wave":2,"themes":["retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-a4479f8df8","kind":"quote","text":"Each network upgrade added to a transmission owner’s revenue requirement while these questions remain open embeds another cost shift","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","src":["udadvocates0721"],"date":"2026-07-21","wave":2,"themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-bc42ae41b8","kind":"quote","text":"Unjust and unreasonable wholesale transmission cost shifts from large loads in PJM can manifest through unjust and unreasonable cost allocation of both [Regional Transmission Expansion Plan] projects and supplemental projects","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","src":["udadvocates0721"],"date":"2026-07-21","wave":2,"themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-37c8628ea8","kind":"quote","text":"That move violates the law. PJM's Tariff guarantees cost shifts due to transmission for data centers. Only FERC can fix that. And it must.","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","src":["peskoe0720"],"date":"2026-07-20","wave":2,"themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-d291442195","kind":"quote","text":"It did not order the utilities to charge those upgrade costs to data centers. They are still spread to everyone.","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","src":["peskoe0723"],"date":"2026-07-23","wave":2,"themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-aee256e842","kind":"quote","text":"We therefore dismiss the requests for rehearing, which concern a Commission action that is not final and that will be succeeded by further Commission action.","title":"State advocates: transparency is not a remedy","src":["rehearing0921"],"date":"2026-09-21","wave":2,"themes":["retail","procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-b56dd6f253","kind":"quote","text":"several provisions of the proposed Tariff appear to place obligations directly on Eligible New Large Loads, which are, for the most part, retail customers","title":"FERC draws the retail line under PJM's curtail-first service","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-6827ded108","kind":"quote","text":"the proposed compensation framework appears to establish a retail rate","title":"FERC draws the retail line under PJM's curtail-first service","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-5a4ad3ad5f","kind":"quote","text":"we strongly encourage states to continue their important work on this issue","title":"FERC draws the retail line under PJM's curtail-first service","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-8c87a20044","kind":"quote","text":"honoring the concept that existing electricity customers should not be unfairly burdened by new, energy intensive customers","title":"FERC draws the retail line under PJM's curtail-first service","src":["udive1012"],"date":"2026-10-12","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-476ae94865","kind":"quote","text":"the latest development is positive for existing generation/IPPs","title":"FERC draws the retail line under PJM's curtail-first service","src":["udive1012"],"date":"2026-10-12","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-dc6b4d3a18","kind":"quote","text":"That’s not only economically inefficient, but also likely to prevent supply and demand from coming back into balance.","title":"Rosner: curtail data centers last, and only after demand response","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-cf281acfbe","kind":"quote","text":"We need state regulators to establish clear rules for which customers should be curtailed first when circumstances require it.","title":"Rosner: curtail data centers last, and only after demand response","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-74574f5932","kind":"quote","text":"such measures should only be taken when necessary to keep the system reliable, after voluntary demand response resources have been exhausted","title":"Rosner: curtail data centers last, and only after demand response","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-651fb3b5ef","kind":"quote","text":"in an emergency, data centers not serving critical functions should be shut off before ordinary customers","title":"Rosner: curtail data centers last, and only after demand response","src":["llorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-a75a7ac660","kind":"quote","text":"PJM is reviewing the order and intends to work quickly to address the Commission’s remaining concerns.","title":"PJM answers two setbacks with refilings, not rehearing","src":["pjmil0930"],"date":"2026-09-30","wave":3,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-bc40db0854","kind":"quote","text":"PJM submitted a motion for abeyance of the paper hearing on October 12 and notified FERC that it plans to submit a new 205 filing to FERC on October 29th","title":"PJM answers two setbacks with refilings, not rehearing","src":["pjmmc1022"],"date":"2026-10-15","wave":3,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-2478c30987","kind":"quote","text":"Remove the opt out for muni/co-ops.","title":"PJM answers two setbacks with refilings, not rehearing","src":["pjmmc1022"],"date":"2026-10-15","wave":3,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-f42cfbabfa","kind":"quote","text":"The PJM Transmission Owners have committed to submit their proposed revisions to address the concerns identified by the June 18, 2026 order issued in Docket No. EL26-67-000","title":"Nov 16 is turning into a §205 date","src":["pjmto1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":[],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-a935b9fd0a","kind":"quote","text":"The ISO and the Participating Transmission Owners Administrative Committee (PTO AC) were granted an abeyance and will develop a Section 205 filing to be submitted by November 16, 2026.","title":"Nov 16 is turning into a §205 date","src":["isone1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-61feaf077d","kind":"quote","text":"LARS provides MISO with a targeted tool to address the intersection of significant large load additions,","title":"Nov 16 is turning into a §205 date","src":["udive1005"],"date":"2026-10-05","wave":3,"themes":[],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"]},{"id":"quote-334d1d9a64","kind":"quote","text":"This type of operation is unsustainable and needs to be addressed,","title":"Nov 16 is turning into a §205 date","src":["pjmil0910"],"date":"2026-09-10","wave":3,"themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-7b514e7ec6","kind":"quote","text":"PJM is saying very clearly to the data centers, 'We will no longer buy power on your behalf,'","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","src":["wypr0826"],"date":"2026-08-26","wave":3,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-9c6a59da53","kind":"quote","text":"If the data centers don't show up in those zones, then it'll be the existing customers, ordinary households included, that will pay those costs.","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","src":["wypr0826"],"date":"2026-08-26","wave":3,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-1a814a2ac5","kind":"quote","text":"This Commission will not be forced into accepting a deeply flawed, eleventh-hour procurement mechanism with billion-dollar implications for consumers.","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","src":["backstop0929"],"date":"2026-09-29","wave":3,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-58aed52948","kind":"quote","text":"Existing customers should not be left paying costs attributable to new demand.","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","src":["backstop0929"],"date":"2026-09-29","wave":3,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-af0c5ac09b","kind":"quote","text":"Surprising! FERC has issues with the cost allocation framework that determined who pays for this data center capacity auction.","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","src":["peskoe0930"],"date":"2026-09-30","wave":3,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-e37da6a91f","kind":"quote","text":"the Energy Department supports having large electricity users pay for reliable new power generation","title":"The backstop bill: who pays for capacity bought for new load","src":["doe1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-21d0da5ed6","kind":"quote","text":"In doing so, it appears to be putting more pressure on states to find answers.","title":"After Oct 9, the states hold the pen on curtailment","src":["dennisx1012"],"date":"2026-10-12","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-b73a88d5e5","kind":"quote","text":"It does slow down the development of these more innovative bilateral mechanisms to solve some of these problems","title":"After Oct 9, the states hold the pen on curtailment","src":["latitude1013"],"date":"2026-10-13","wave":3,"themes":["flex","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-f75329f2b3","kind":"quote","text":"At a minimum, PJM's rules must require data centers to bring their own supply, and not allow them to raise electricity costs or cause blackouts","title":"After Oct 9, the states hold the pen on curtailment","src":["bisnow1014"],"date":"2026-10-14","wave":3,"themes":["cost","flex","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-e08ce5ad11","kind":"quote","text":"To the extent state regulatory actions are not sufficient to protect consumers, the Commission has an obligation to seriously consider expanding the cost recovery framework it established in the Large Load Show Cause Orders","title":"After Oct 9, the states hold the pen on curtailment","src":["dukeorder1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","wave":3,"themes":["cost","flex","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-fa1309d8c8","kind":"quote","text":"PJM filed an amended proposal to FERC with the goal of commencing a Reliability Backstop Procurement on March 1, 2027.","title":"The backstop comes back, and the rehearing requests hedge","src":["pjmil1029"],"date":"2026-10-29","wave":3,"themes":["adequacy","procedure"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-598a35e6ac","kind":"quote","text":"Through this section 205 filing, PJM has addressed the issues set for paper hearing in the September 29 Order.","title":"The backstop comes back, and the rehearing requests hedge","src":["pjmrbp1029"],"date":"2026-10-29","wave":3,"themes":["adequacy","procedure"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-fb798768f3","kind":"quote","text":"permits the unmitigated exercise of seller-side market power","title":"The backstop comes back, and the rehearing requests hedge","src":["jcareh1029"],"date":"2026-10-29","wave":3,"themes":["adequacy","procedure"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-ca9612bb95","kind":"quote","text":"submit a revised section 205 filing that supersedes its earlier section 205 filing and may moot the need for rehearing of the Order","title":"The backstop comes back, and the rehearing requests hedge","src":["toreh1029"],"date":"2026-10-29","wave":3,"themes":["adequacy","procedure"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"quote-5f28ce02aa","kind":"quote","text":"Our response to the FERC order to show cause will improve planning and operational awareness of new large loads on the system, prevent cost-shifting, encourage flexibility and expedite large load energization","title":"CAISO's board signs off, and PJM books its consultation","src":["appa1028"],"date":"2026-10-28","wave":3,"themes":["cost","flex"],"groups":["sc-pjm","sc-caiso"]},{"id":"quote-e0ce4d1444","kind":"quote","text":"We look forward to our ongoing collaboration with our participating transmission owners, state energy agencies and industry stakeholders to make sure we stay ahead of the curve on interconnecting large loads without compromising reliability or placing an undue burden on consumers","title":"CAISO's board signs off, and PJM books its consultation","src":["appa1028"],"date":"2026-10-28","wave":3,"themes":["adequacy","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm","sc-caiso"]},{"id":"quote-1a966f3a5c","kind":"quote","text":"PJM staff will consult with stakeholders on plans to submit a suite of section 205 filings to address Tariff revisions to address opportunities identified in the Large Load Show Cause Order and related reforms.","title":"CAISO's board signs off, and PJM books its consultation","src":["pjmmc1105"],"date":"2026-10-29","wave":3,"themes":[],"groups":["sc-pjm","sc-caiso"]},{"id":"quote-7a1eb9f996","kind":"quote","text":"LARS cannot become a general alternative to the existing [generation interconnection queue] or provide preferential access to transmission capacity without adequate safeguards for existing queue participants and transmission customers","title":"MISO's fast lane for large loads splits its own footprint","src":["udive1026"],"date":"2026-10-26","wave":3,"themes":["study"],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"]},{"id":"quote-2bb56fc7df","kind":"quote","text":"The lack of cost shifting protections is especially concerning because, despite the benefits — especially speed to power — that LARS would promote, it is structured in a way that threatens Google’s ability to meet its foundational commitment to ensuring that other retail customers do not bear the costs of serving its data centers, including through the Ratepayer Protection Pledge","title":"MISO's fast lane for large loads splits its own footprint","src":["udive1026"],"date":"2026-10-26","wave":3,"themes":["study","cost","retail"],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"]},{"id":"quote-d617c9974b","kind":"quote","text":"In MISO’s primarily vertically integrated footprint, establishing the LSE as the sole LARS application facilitator narrows the pool of supply that can gain access to expedited Large Load Generation Interconnection Agreements ... to only those projects that are effectively approved by the LSE","title":"MISO's fast lane for large loads splits its own footprint","src":["udive1026"],"date":"2026-10-26","wave":3,"themes":[],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"]},{"id":"quote-3af461e222","kind":"quote","text":"While the Order calls for improvements to large load integration, it does not require PJM to overhaul its entire generation interconnection queue.","title":"The customers ask PJM not to answer with a queue overhaul","src":["cebadcc1002"],"date":"2026-10-02","wave":3,"themes":["study"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-8b78304b25","kind":"quote","text":"PJM has not demonstrated that this proposal will deliver the speed, certainty, or transparency envisioned by the Show Cause Order.","title":"The customers ask PJM not to answer with a queue overhaul","src":["cebadcc1002"],"date":"2026-10-02","wave":3,"themes":["study"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"quote-88b3fc3f76","kind":"quote","text":"New and large, in some cases gigantic, Data Centers are mandated by me to make their own electricity, and give or sell any excess back to the local community, or grid","title":"Bring your own power, as a presidential line","src":["bisnow1021"],"date":"2026-10-21","wave":3,"themes":["proximate"],"groups":[]},{"id":"quote-1557fed060","kind":"quote","text":"When push comes to shove, Trump would rather you lose power than the data center companies lose a cent of profit.","title":"Bring your own power, as a presidential line","src":["vanhollenx1021"],"date":"2026-10-21","wave":3,"themes":["proximate"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-0d9bbe5eaf","kind":"voice","who":"CSIS Energy Security and Climate Change Program","affil":"Majkut, Garcia, Gilchrist and Jaffal","text":"Scored all six operators against the five reform categories three weeks after the orders and found the gap wide: of 30 matrix boxes, “only 6 are plausibly sufficient,” and “none of the six grid operators are likely to receive a clear pass through the five reforms.” Reads SPP as proof the targets are reachable inside the existing framework rather than evidence the bar is too high.","src":["csis"],"date":"2026-07-09","themes":[],"groups":["sc-spp"]},{"id":"voice-23b63f71ca","kind":"voice","who":"Juan Arteaga","affil":"Enverus Intelligence Research","text":"Graded regional readiness two days before FERC granted the abeyances and put SPP alone at the front: “the only region with FERC-approved large-load tariffs.” The uncomfortable part is the mismatch he names, that “large-load development is concentrating in markets where key interconnection and cost-allocation rules are still evolving.”","src":["enverus"],"date":"2026-08-12","themes":["cost","procedure"],"groups":["sc-spp"]},{"id":"voice-28a587e156","kind":"voice","who":"Travis Kavulla","affil":"American Affairs · NRG","text":"Wants grid access priced, not rationed: borrow the natural-gas ‘open season’ so large loads bid for transferable interconnection rights instead of queuing first-come, first-served. Reads the orders’ cost-causation push as a step toward that, but short of market pricing.","src":["kavulla","kavullali"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-95cf0f2eae","kind":"voice","who":"Devin Hartman","affil":"R Street Institute","text":"Supportive of the design: calls the action ‘far more substantively ambitious than the ANOPR,’ with region-specific §206 pathways more effective than a uniform rule. Faults it mainly for leaving out non-RTO areas, which he says have the worst interconnection practices.","src":["utilitydive"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-edaff64937","kind":"voice","who":"Sierra Club","affil":"environmental advocacy","text":"Cautiously positive: the announcement is ‘responsive to Sierra Club’s requests on several fronts, including protecting consumers from costs incurred by large loads,’ with the full orders still to be reviewed.","src":["insideclimate"],"date":"2026-06-23","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-eae76df192","kind":"voice","who":"Southern Environmental Law Center","affil":"Nick Guidi, senior attorney","text":"Unconvinced by the cost guardrail: ‘There’s no real quarantining of the cost caused by data centers to those data centers,’ so it gets ‘spread throughout the whole customer base.’ Wanted more federal standardization than the orders deliver.","src":["insideclimate"],"date":"2026-06-23","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-9f0da96da4","kind":"voice","who":"Public Citizen","affil":"consumer watchdog","text":"The most skeptical: pressed FERC for a temporary moratorium on new data-center interconnections until NERC reliability alerts are resolved, and warns against weakening state authority over data centers.","src":["publiccitizen"],"date":"2026-06-23","themes":["adequacy","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-8561e216fb","kind":"voice","who":"Mona Dajani","affil":"Cooley LLP","text":"Reads the six-order route as the end of any national template: ‘The era of one national standard for data center interconnection is over before it began.’ What replaces it, she says, is ‘six regional answers to the same question, decided on six different timelines’: clarity within each region, fragmentation across them.","src":["utilitydive"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-c4bd05d9f5","kind":"voice","who":"Neil Chatterjee","affil":"former FERC chairman","text":"Calls it ‘a very FERC-y approach’ and says the design targets the core challenges ‘on a region by region basis on a tight timeline.’ He approves of the region-specific §206 route as faster and more legally defensible than one national rule.","src":["heatmap","eenewsferc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-35f1ad7653","kind":"voice","who":"Jennifer Danis","affil":"Institute for Policy Integrity","text":"Reads the six-order route as built to survive appeal: a § 206 show cause yields ‘a much more robust and detailed administrative record’ tailored to each RTO, which she says ‘reduces future litigation entry points’ versus a single national rule.","src":["bloomberglaw"],"date":"2026-06-27","themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-5a7c299235","kind":"voice","who":"Matthew Christiansen","affil":"Wilson Sonsini","text":"On the federalism question: reads the orders as a legitimate exercise of FERC's transmission jurisdiction rather than an intrusion on the states. Says he doesn't think ‘anything that FERC proposed to do’ encroaches on state authority, so the state-versus-federal challenge critics expect starts from a narrow opening.","src":["bloomberglaw"],"date":"2026-06-27","themes":["retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-00be3c0552","kind":"voice","who":"Jigar Shah","affil":"Deploy Action · former DOE Loan Programs Office","text":"Reads the orders as a clever jurisdictional move: region-by-region § 206 orders avoid the federal-overreach fight while targeting the ‘speed to power’ bottleneck. His caveat is that FERC is not solving the price problem directly; data centers are an accelerant on a strained system, and the missing piece is a public capacity-and-demand picture.","src":["jigarferc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["study","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-eec10c40d5","kind":"voice","who":"Ben Inskeep","affil":"Citizens Action Coalition of Indiana","text":"Brings the consumer-advocate reliability lens: skeptical that data centers need a faster path to the grid, but encouraged that FERC is taking up large-load interconnection. His broader warning is that standards must protect ratepayers from negative stability, reliability and resiliency impacts as data centers proliferate.","src":["benferc","bennerc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-2c4a38d326","kind":"voice","who":"Aniruddh Mohan","affil":"The Brattle Group","text":"The post-order technical voice: flags the Maryland/PJM complaint as part of ‘increasing scrutiny on who pays’ for transmission to connect data centers, connecting the orders' cost-causation frame to a live regional fight.","src":["mohanx"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"voice-e7bd9cc079","kind":"voice","who":"Arthur Haubenstock","affil":"Equinix","text":"The data-center-operator reality check: says the industry’s power needs collide with local grid impacts, backup-generation limits, and the real constraints behind ‘BYOP / BYONCE’ flexibility. Useful counterweight to the idea that every large load can simply curtail or bring its own supply.","src":["arthurwm"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex","proximate"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-659fb0447d","kind":"voice","who":"Jeff Dennis","affil":"Electricity Customer Alliance · Coefficient Policy Experts","text":"The customer-affordability voice: supports FERC's move because it accelerates pathways for new large loads while reinforcing that those loads should bear the costs incurred to serve them, with more transparency and state coordination. In IEEE's coverage, he says FERC is threading the needle between speed-to-power and public concern over broader bill impacts. In October he read FERC's rejection of PJM's curtail-first service as putting more pressure on the states to find answers, and noted the order did not take up PJM's argument that the service matched SPP's approved CHILLS construct.","src":["jeffdennis","ieee","dennisx1012"],"date":"2026-10-12","themes":["study","cost","flex","retail"],"groups":["spp-chills","sc-pjm","sc-spp"]},{"id":"voice-25d88e46f7","kind":"voice","who":"Ben Schifman","affil":"Institute for Progress","text":"The security-and-reliability skeptic: reads FERC's large-load action as meaningful interconnection reform, but flags that ‘security gaps remain’ as the grid absorbs more data-center demand.","src":["xschifman"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-cc2e44cf6f","kind":"voice","who":"Simon Mahan","affil":"Southern Renewable Energy Association","text":"The Southeast planning voice: argues that manufacturers and data centers are bringing jobs to the region, but without a modern transmission system ‘we risk higher energy prices, power shortages, and lost economic opportunities.’ His point sharpens the non-RTO coverage gap the orders do not directly solve.","src":["simonmahan"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-ac60c7739a","kind":"voice","who":"Briggs White","affil":"Keystone Ascent","text":"The buildout-and-supply-chain voice: sees hyperscaler demand as a physical infrastructure buildout, with co-location now assumed and emergency data-center load reduction ‘ripe for deeper industry analysis.’ His warning is that turbine, transformer and interconnection lead times force regulators to keep costs manageable while maintaining reliability.","src":["briggswhite"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost","colo","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-colocation"]},{"id":"voice-acf143883f","kind":"voice","who":"Heather McGeory","affil":"CoreWeave","text":"A hyperscaler power-procurement voice: says the conversation has moved from megawatts to gigawatts for individual data centers. Her presence in the discourse reinforces that AI infrastructure demand is no longer an abstract forecast; it is a siting, procurement and grid-modernization problem now.","src":["heathermcgeory"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["registry"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-7a259761d6","kind":"voice","who":"Varun Sivaram","affil":"Emerald AI","text":"The software-flexibility voice: argues data centers can become grid assets, not just fixed loads, by dynamically adjusting demand to relieve grid stress while maintaining performance.","src":["varunsivaram"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-c47809dc88","kind":"voice","who":"Tim Latimer","affil":"Fervo Energy","text":"The firm-clean-power voice: Fervo's Google data-center deals show the supply-side counterpart to FERC's load-interconnection push: hyperscalers are also trying to procure 24/7 geothermal power near the places where AI load is growing.","src":["timlatimer"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-c52e84c786","kind":"voice","who":"Jeff Bladen","affil":"Verrus","text":"The asset-backed-flexibility operator: argues a flexible data center can be a grid asset, not only a consumer, and that physical storage, controls and telemetry make that different from old demand-response programs.","src":["jeffbladen"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex","registry"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-5fceea3089","kind":"voice","who":"Jamie Nolan","affil":"Energy Empire · Nolan Strategic Communications","text":"The public-narrative voice: frames data centers as a guardrails problem, not a binary good-or-bad story. Done wrong, they are the Loudoun County cautionary tale; done right, she argues they can become a local-bill ally rather than the villain driving bills up.","src":["jamienolan"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-f0596d1176","kind":"voice","who":"Lucia Tian","affil":"Google","text":"The Google advanced-energy voice: frames AI's power demand as a race to secure carbon-free power through geothermal, nuclear, long-duration storage and grid optimization. That makes the hyperscaler story about procuring new clean supply, not only asking grid operators for faster interconnection.","src":["luciatian"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-3b57d29343","kind":"voice","who":"Sara Axelrod","affil":"Crusoe","text":"The Crusoe policy voice: says AI compute is making energy the defining constraint and competitive advantage for data infrastructure, and frames Crusoe's answer as energy-first infrastructure that co-locates compute with abundant or stranded power.","src":["saraaxelrod"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["colo"],"groups":["pjm-colocation"]},{"id":"voice-b23d1ddb21","kind":"voice","who":"Chase Lochmiller","affil":"Crusoe","text":"The AI-campus builder voice: says developers are pairing data-center growth with behind-the-meter generation and siting near stranded or underused power. His Stargate work makes the large-load debate concrete: build compute where power can actually be delivered.","src":["chaselochmiller"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["colo"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-7b33bab529","kind":"voice","who":"Cully Cavness","affil":"Crusoe","text":"The bridge-to-grid voice: describes gas as a bridge to grid power at Abilene / Stargate, showing how AI data centers are turning into power-development projects before the grid can catch up.","src":["cullycavness"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-74eff80a94","kind":"voice","who":"Amanda Peterson Corio","affil":"Google","text":"The Google grid-utilization voice: says the sector has to find solutions that better utilize the system. Google works on data-center flexibility, but she also argues it can be faster and more cost effective to pay other customers to shift load because idle chips are expensive.","src":["amandapc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost","flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-468ac0e42c","kind":"voice","who":"Charles Hua","affil":"PowerLines","text":"The utility-regulation voice: calls data centers arguably the most important topic in energy, but warns that blaming every bill increase on data centers is too simple. His focus is utility incentives, transparency and whether regulators center consumers as the grid is rebuilt.","src":["charleshua"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-ddda8cfe3e","kind":"voice","who":"Chris Gillett","affil":"Works in Progress","text":"The interconnection-mechanics voice: argues America has enough electricity in aggregate; the hard part is getting new data centers and power plants studied and connected to the grid, and flexible connection rights could let some loads move faster.","src":["chrisgillett"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-615ff65292","kind":"voice","who":"Shanu Mathew","affil":"Energy, power and infrastructure investor","text":"The capital-markets voice: focuses on energized GW rather than announced demand, with power constraints and political resistance as the variables that decide which data-center projects become real. On FERC, he reads the reform as fixing slow, inconsistent interconnection rules and making full upgrade-cost responsibility part of the bargain.","src":["shanumathew"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-b786aa572d","kind":"voice","who":"Josh Levi","affil":"Data Center Coalition","text":"The data-center trade-association voice: pushes back on data centers being singled out for grid impacts and argues the sector needs utilities, grid operators and policymakers to grow infrastructure and recognize data centers as part of the modern economy.","src":["joshlevi"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-412e05cf0e","kind":"voice","who":"Cy McGeady","affil":"Equinix · former CSIS","text":"The Equinix energy-policy voice: argues electricity supply is a bottleneck for U.S. AI dominance, with data-center investment moving toward regions where generation, permitting and land-use conditions allow rapid buildout.","src":["cymcgeady"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-04fb1eaebe","kind":"voice","who":"Cy McNeill","affil":"Data Center Coalition","text":"The DCC federal-affairs voice: works the transmission, siting, permitting and energy-market advocacy lane for the data-center industry. Distinct from Cy McGeady at Equinix, McNeill represents the association's federal policy posture.","src":["cymcneill"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-e8191f7431","kind":"voice","who":"David Young","affil":"Equinix · Data Center Coalition","text":"The data-center federal-policy voice: represents the Equinix/DCC lane in the coalition's Federal Policy Leadership Advisory Council, reinforcing that the industry is organizing around federal power, permitting and grid-access questions.","src":["davidyoung"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-093e96e068","kind":"voice","who":"Tag Greason","affil":"QTS Data Centers","text":"The data-center capacity-delivery voice: from the operator side, emphasizes hyperscale AI demand and the bottlenecks that determine whether capacity can actually be delivered, not just announced.","src":["taggreason"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-3be4376b63","kind":"voice","who":"Larry Gasteiger","affil":"WIRES (transmission trade group)","text":"From the transmission-builder camp: reads the show cause route as the faster path, since ‘going through the show cause process makes a lot of sense, because you don't have all of the time lags associated with a lengthy rulemaking process.’","src":["bloomberglaw"],"date":"2026-06-27","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-8493ecfd37","kind":"voice","who":"Ari Peskoe","affil":"Harvard Law School Electricity Law Initiative","text":"The legal critic from the consumer side. Backs the four-state advocates' rehearing argument against the PJM order: ‘That move violates the law. PJM’s Tariff guarantees cost shifts due to transmission for data centers. Only FERC can fix that.’ Days later he added that FERC ordered no change to how utilities share upgrade costs, so those costs are still spread to everyone, the case his April op-ed made against FERC's transmission-pricing policy. In September he called FERC's objection to the backstop's cost allocation surprising.","src":["peskoe0720","peskoe0723","peskoe0930","peskoe"],"date":"2026-09-30","themes":["cost","adequacy","retail","procedure"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"voice-ca4b9c2e1a","kind":"voice","who":"David S. Lapp","affil":"Maryland People’s Counsel","text":"The state consumer advocate's case against the PJM order. His office's July 17 filing, joined by the Ohio, Delaware and Illinois advocates, argues the order exposes data-center cost shifts without fixing the PJM rules that cause them, and that it ‘erroneously assumes State regulators have authority that they do not clearly have.’ On PJM's backstop proposal he warned that if forecast data centers never arrive, existing customers, households included, pay the cost.","src":["mdopc0717","udadvocates0721","wypr0826"],"date":"2026-08-26","themes":["cost","registry","adequacy","retail"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"voice-e5b8267683","kind":"voice","who":"National Association of Regulatory Utility Commissioners","affil":"Ann Rendahl, NARUC President (Washington UTC)","text":"The state regulators' first word, issued the day of the orders. Reserved judgment until the orders were posted, welcomed the commissioners' ‘recognizing the critical role of states and state regulators,’ and said states remain committed to allocating costs appropriately across customer classes.","src":["naruc0618"],"date":"2026-06-18","themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-068079f247","kind":"voice","who":"RMI","affil":"Sarah Wang and Tyler Farrell","text":"The definition-gap read. FERC accepted SPP's tiered definition (over 10 MW at 69 kV or below, over 50 MW above it), while ‘FERC’s suggested definition for other RTOs does not address facilities interconnecting at or below 69 kV,’ which leaves each operator to decide whether to write its own.","src":["rmi0706"],"date":"2026-07-06","themes":["registry"],"groups":["sc-spp"]},{"id":"voice-b850b4a51a","kind":"voice","who":"Claire Lang-Ree","affil":"NRDC Sustainable FERC Project","text":"The environmental-advocate case for making data centers bring their own power. In August she welcomed PJM's backstop proposal as PJM declining to keep buying power on data centers' behalf. After FERC rejected PJM's curtail-first service on Oct 9, NRDC criticized the ruling and she urged PJM to refile: ‘At a minimum, PJM’s rules must require data centers to bring their own supply, and not allow them to raise electricity costs or cause blackouts.’","src":["bisnow1014","wypr0826"],"date":"2026-10-14","themes":["cost","flex","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"voice-bef0e91a2d","kind":"voice","who":"Tyler Norris","affil":"Duke University Nicholas Institute","text":"The researcher behind the flexible-load headroom estimate the flexible-service reform leans on. Four days after the orders he thanked FERC for its bipartisan leadership, ‘especially on establishing new flexible service options for large loads.’","src":["norrisx0622","norris"],"date":"2026-06-23","themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"voice-c5d8d339db","kind":"voice","who":"Chris Wright","affil":"U.S. Secretary of Energy","text":"The directing agency, declaring victory: DOE applauds the orders as speed-to-power that still protects consumers. Secretary Wright frames them as the Administration working to ‘remove barriers, accelerate development’ and to deliver on ‘President Trump's Ratepayer Protection Pledge.’ In October DOE backed FERC's backstop order for PJM, with Wright saying large electricity users should pay for the new generation built to serve them.","src":["doeApplaud","doe1009"],"date":"2026-10-09","themes":["study","cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"]},{"id":"voice-328ebacebc","kind":"voice","who":"Elliot Mainzer","affil":"California ISO (President and CEO)","text":"Frames CAISO's Large Loads proposal, advanced by its Board on Oct 28 for a filing by Nov 16, as the show cause answer itself: ‘Our response to the FERC order to show cause will improve planning and operational awareness of new large loads on the system, prevent cost-shifting, encourage flexibility and expedite large load energization.’ Pitches it as staying ahead of large-load growth without ‘placing an undue burden on consumers.’","src":["appa1028"],"date":"2026-10-28","themes":["cost","flex"],"groups":["sc-caiso"]},{"id":"voice-59744e890b","kind":"voice","who":"Organization of MISO States","affil":"regional state committee for MISO","text":"The states' check on MISO's fast lane for large-load generation. In comments on LARS (ER27-25), as Utility Dive quoted them, it draws the line at a bypass: ‘LARS cannot become a general alternative to the existing [generation interconnection queue] or provide preferential access to transmission capacity without adequate safeguards for existing queue participants and transmission customers.’","src":["udive1026"],"date":"2026-10-26","themes":["study","retail"],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"]},{"id":"voice-dbce54064b","kind":"voice","who":"Corporate Energy Buyers Association and Data Center Coalition","affil":"large energy buyers and data-center operators (joint letter)","text":"The customers the show cause order is meant to serve, asking PJM not to answer it with a queue overhaul. Their Oct 2 letter to PJM's Board: ‘PJM has not demonstrated that this proposal will deliver the speed, certainty, or transparency envisioned by the Show Cause Order.’ They want either a direct answer to the 60-to-90-day study directive or a narrower filing now.","src":["cebadcc1002"],"date":"2026-10-02","themes":["study"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"voice-5b01973d1c","kind":"voice","who":"Chris Van Hollen","affil":"U.S. Senator (D-Md.)","text":"Puts FERC alongside the White House as failing ratepayers on data centers: ‘Clearly, this admin & FERC are not protecting consumers.’ Pitches his Power for the People Act as the fix (X, Oct 21).","src":["vanhollenx1021"],"date":"2026-10-21","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"consensus-95351c2ffe","kind":"consensus","text":"‘Historic’ / ‘landmark’: the framing is near-universal across trade press and the bar, with FERC asserting itself on load interconnection in an area it historically left alone.","src":["akin","rew","powermag"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"consensus-0461fdbadb","kind":"consensus","text":"Speed via procedure: § 206 show cause beats a multi-year NOPR; commentators read it as DOE’s April 30 deadline operationalized across all six markets at once.","src":["aaf","rtoinsider"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"consensus-463754d087","kind":"consensus","text":"Regional flexibility reads as a deliberate design choice: SPP and PJM are treated as proactive leaders, others told to catch up without a uniform template.","src":["powermag","latitude"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":["flex"],"groups":["sc-pjm","sc-spp"]},{"id":"consensus-22402fcbeb","kind":"consensus","text":"Flexibility is becoming the price of speed: practitioners read curtailable / flexible service as the most valuable near-term lever to connect faster, turning a load’s willingness to limit usage into bargaining power.","src":["duanemorris","briggswhite","varunsivaram","jeffbladen","norrisx0622"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"consensus-426586c7f6","kind":"consensus","text":"Built to survive appeal: legal analysts read the six tailored § 206 orders as deliberately litigation-resistant. A detailed, region-specific administrative record offers fewer openings to challenge than a single national rule, and it skips the multi-year lag of a rulemaking.","src":["bloomberglaw","heatmap","eenewsferc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["procedure"],"groups":[]},{"id":"consensus-330c9c175d","kind":"consensus","text":"The public headline has escaped the energy-law lane: TechCrunch, AP, Tom's Hardware and Reddit all translate the orders as a data-center fast lane, even when they disagree on whether the real bottleneck is interconnection paperwork, physical power supply, or household bills.","src":["techcrunch","apdata","tomhardware","jigarferc","redditecon","reddittech"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"consensus-07dccc2db4","kind":"consensus","text":"Law-firm alerts read the orders as staying inside FERC's existing lines: no new or expanded jurisdiction over retail or co-located load, and a push for each region to propose its own §205 fix before FERC imposes one, with a warning that abeyances would not be granted reflexively.","src":["hklaw0623","hunton0626","baker0630"],"date":"2026-06-30","themes":["colo","retail","procedure"],"groups":["pjm-colocation"]},{"id":"consensus-102e25f604","kind":"consensus","text":"X is treating the 60-day response clock as the live market story: energy/security analysts and infrastructure accounts are watching whether the RTO filings answer the real scarcity question, not just whether they satisfy FERC's process.","src":["xschifman","xnearco","xhalcyon"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-219a11bd33","kind":"friction","text":"Cost allocation is the central fight: how aggressively must large loads pre-fund and backstop upgrades, and over what crediting term? The ANOPR explicitly left crediting open.","src":["utilitydive","doe403"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"friction-59e90f80d3","kind":"friction","text":"Speed vs. rigor: the orders set a 45-day window to request abeyance of up to 90 days (NYISO P 42), which prevents indefinite stalling but compresses stakeholder process; the mandatory alternative-transmission-technology evaluation could itself add study time.","src":["e12","substack"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":["study","procedure"],"groups":["sc-nyiso"]},{"id":"friction-126370fff0","kind":"friction","text":"Coverage-gap questions: commenters flag that some transmission owners (e.g., Nebraska Public Power District, Great River Energy) appear absent from the published respondent lists, a completeness question to watch.","src":["substack"],"date":"2026-06-22","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-a98545a967","kind":"friction","text":"The cost-allocation fight is already concrete and pending: Maryland’s ratepayer advocate, backed by 80 state lawmakers (June 17, 2026), says PJM’s tariff lets data-center transmission costs ‘leak’ across zones, ‘an unjust subsidy for that data center load’, and pegs Maryland’s exposure near $1.6 billion over a decade (FERC comment window extended to July 27, 2026).","src":["mdopc","utilitydive"],"date":"2026-06-24","themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"friction-78b1f54583","kind":"friction","text":"The state-authority premise is contested: Maryland's People's Counsel and advocates from Delaware, Illinois, Ohio and Pennsylvania argue the order leans on states to protect retail customers from costs that PJM's federally approved allocation sends them; states, they say, have no authority to move costs between transmission customers. Ari Peskoe backs them.","src":["mdopc0717","udadvocates0721","peskoe0720"],"date":"2026-07-21","themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"friction-b218db6dde","kind":"friction","text":"Definition gap: RMI notes that the large-load definition FERC suggests for the other RTOs (over 50 MW, taking service above 69 kV) does not reach facilities at or below 69 kV, which SPP's accepted definition does.","src":["rmi0706"],"date":"2026-07-06","themes":["registry"],"groups":["sc-spp"]},{"id":"friction-2a9e331d37","kind":"friction","text":"Fragmentation risk: six regional answers on six different timelines give multi-region developers clarity within a market but a patchwork across them, and fresh diligence for near-term deals.","src":["utilitydive","duanemorris"],"date":"2026-06-24","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-1a3677df57","kind":"friction","text":"The affordability backstop is split: FERC guards against cost-shifting among transmission customers but says preventing cost-shifting among retail customers is the states’ job, so the household-bill fight moves to state regulators even as the orders’ federal reach over load interconnection invites a jurisdictional challenge.","src":["thehill","bloomberglaw"],"date":"2026-06-27","themes":["cost","adequacy","retail"],"groups":["pjm-backstop"]},{"id":"friction-3286a98879","kind":"friction","text":"Fast lane vs. power supply: TechCrunch and Reddit frame the order as faster access to the grid, but AP and tech press stress the harder constraint: data-center construction is outrunning new generation, transmission upgrades and local consent.","src":["techcrunch","apdata","tomhardware","redditecon"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-db6d21b558","kind":"friction","text":"Reliability risk is now part of the same conversation: consumer advocates point to NERC's Level 3 data-center alert as the reason fast interconnection needs enforceable modeling, operating and ratepayer-protection standards.","src":["benferc","bennerc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost","adequacy"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-733c0cbd59","kind":"friction","text":"Developer reality check: Equinix's Arthur Haubenstock argues that data centers often cannot simply behave as flexible grid loads, and CoreWeave's Heather McGeory shows the scale has moved from megawatts to gigawatts. That complicates any policy story that treats curtailment or on-site supply as an easy fix.","src":["arthurwm","heathermcgeory"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-d44518879f","kind":"friction","text":"The buildout is colliding with supply-chain physics: Briggs White points to 3- to 5-year gas-turbine lead times, 3- to 4-year transformer lead times and interconnection delays, so the FERC clock is only one part of a much larger delivery bottleneck.","src":["briggswhite"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-acae5e3fad","kind":"friction","text":"Firm clean supply is the other half of the answer: Tim Latimer's Fervo work with Google and Lucia Tian's advanced-energy portfolio show why hyperscalers are pairing interconnection reform with geothermal, storage, nuclear and grid-optimization procurement, not only demand flexibility.","src":["timlatimer","luciatian"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["flex"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-684aa9e2a2","kind":"friction","text":"The hyperscaler line is not monolithic but it is explicit on cost assignment: post-order coverage keeps pairing speed-to-power with large-customer cost responsibility, grid-utilization strategies, and organized federal advocacy from the data-center sector.","src":["amandapc","joshlevi","cymcneill","davidyoung"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["study","cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-455ce84e59","kind":"friction","text":"AI competitiveness has become part of the power-grid argument: Equinix's Cy McGeady frames electricity supply as a bottleneck on U.S. AI dominance, while Tag Greason, Chase Lochmiller, Cully Cavness and Sara Axelrod show the operator side racing to deliver actual data-center capacity with energy attached.","src":["cymcgeady","taggreason","chaselochmiller","cullycavness","saraaxelrod"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-b45f4efe89","kind":"friction","text":"Capital is watching energized gigawatts, not headline announcements: Shanu Mathew frames the investable question as which projects actually get power, interconnection and political permission, with FERC's reform aimed at slow and inconsistent rules.","src":["shanumathew"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-1be83ea84f","kind":"friction","text":"The grid-connection problem has its own mechanics: Chris Gillett argues the country has enough electricity in aggregate, but the bottleneck is getting new loads and generation studied, connected and prioritized; Charles Hua adds that utility regulation and transparency shape whether consumers trust the answer.","src":["chrisgillett","charleshua"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-df36df7bb2","kind":"friction","text":"The public narrative is settling around guardrails, not a simple yes/no on data centers: Jamie Nolan's Energy Empire framing is that data centers can either become the Loudoun County cautionary tale or a local-bill ally, depending on the deal structure.","src":["jamienolan"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-54591673c7","kind":"friction","text":"The Southeast remains the outside-the-RTO pressure point: Simon Mahan argues that data centers and manufacturers need regional transmission planning, not just local patches, or the region risks higher prices and power shortages.","src":["simonmahan"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-968ea5eb22","kind":"friction","text":"Congress may not leave the issue to FERC orders alone: the POWER Up Act would write a 100 MW large-load category into federal law and push standardized transmission-level interconnection rules, while saying state and local siting, generation and retail-rate authority remain intact.","src":["dcdpowerup"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-945fd475a5","kind":"friction","text":"After Oct 9, who pays is open again: one commentator says FERC got the law right and that new large loads should bring their own generation; another, that the largest grid still has no clear path for connecting data centers. A third reads FERC's jurisdictional reasoning as more nuanced than a flat rejection.","src":["enb1013","nyangon1013","konidena1012"],"date":"2026-10-13","themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"friction-c356a884a0","kind":"friction","text":"MISO's answer is splitting its own region: on the Oct 23 comment date for its LARS fast lane, Arkansas, Mississippi, Entergy, Cleco and MISO's transmission owners backed it, while clean-energy trade groups, consumer advocates and Google protested, and the states' committee asked for safeguards for projects already in the queue.","src":["udive1026","elibrary1030"],"date":"2026-10-30","themes":["study","retail"],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"]},{"id":"reception-fee8a738ec","kind":"reception","who":"Data-center developers & hyperscalers","stance":"mixed","text":"Speed-to-power is the prize, but the trade is explicit: large customers are increasingly expected to bring credible capacity commitments, minimum charges, collateral, flexible operations or their own power strategy. Microsoft, Google, DCC, Equinix and operator voices all converge on the same point from different angles: the projects that look real are the ones that can show who pays, who curtails and where deliverable power comes from.","src":["amandapc","joshlevi","cymcgeady","arthurwm"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["study","cost","flex","credit"],"groups":[]},{"id":"reception-db1da5971f","kind":"reception","who":"Transmission owners","stance":"mixed","text":"Named as respondents alongside the RTOs, TOs get clearer cost-allocation rules and a faster §206 lane than a generic rulemaking. The burden is real: they must help build the record, evaluate grid-enhancing technologies before defaulting to conventional upgrades, and answer whether existing tariffs can handle large-load risk without cross-subsidies.","src":["fercFS","bloomberglaw","jeffdennis"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["study","cost"],"groups":[]},{"id":"reception-5dad1e0c9c","kind":"reception","who":"Generators (gas / nuclear)","stance":"positive","text":"Dispatchable and firm-power suppliers benefit from the co-location and proximate-generation frame: a data center that can pair with deliverable generation is easier to study than a naked load request. That helps existing plants near load, but the clean-firm-power lane is also live; geothermal, storage, nuclear and grid-optimization procurement are part of the hyperscaler answer.","src":["utilitydive","timlatimer","luciatian","chaselochmiller"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["study","colo","proximate"],"groups":["pjm-colocation"]},{"id":"reception-05c948c624","kind":"reception","who":"State utility commissions","stance":"positive","text":"The bifurcation gives states wholesale-side transmission-cost visibility while preserving retail cost allocation and siting authority. That matters because the household-bill fight is already concrete: Maryland's ratepayer advocate and state lawmakers are challenging how PJM transmission costs tied to data-center load move across zones.","src":["fercFS","thehill","mdopc","bloomberglaw","naruc0618"],"date":"2026-06-27","themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"reception-b01604baa7","kind":"reception","who":"Consumer and environmental advocates","stance":"mixed","text":"The guardrail camp sees progress but not closure. Sierra Club called the announcement responsive on consumer-protection fronts; SELC wanted stronger federal standardization and clearer quarantine of data-center costs; Public Citizen remains more skeptical, tying fast interconnection to unresolved NERC reliability and state-authority concerns. State consumer advocates went further in July, asking FERC on rehearing to fix PJM's cost allocation itself rather than lean on state authority that, they argue, states do not clearly have.","src":["insideclimate","publiccitizen","benferc","bennerc","mdopc0717","udadvocates0721"],"date":"2026-07-21","themes":["cost","adequacy","retail","procedure"],"groups":["sc-pjm"]},{"id":"reception-9b00007a04","kind":"reception","who":"Grid-flexibility and demand-response providers","stance":"positive","text":"This camp gets a clearer market opening. Flexible-load operators argue that telemetry, controls and dispatchable demand can turn some data centers into grid assets and shorten study timelines. Their caveat is operational proof: the promise only works if the tariff turns flexibility into enforceable performance.","src":["varunsivaram","jeffbladen","norrisx0622"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["study","flex","registry"],"groups":[]},{"id":"reception-3a638aa6d9","kind":"reception","who":"Public power, co-ops and large customers","stance":"mixed","text":"Large customers and public-power interests mostly like faster interconnection when the beneficiary pays. Their worry is that vague cost causation turns into ordinary customers funding speculative upgrades, so the post-order debate keeps coming back to transparency, state coordination and bill-design mechanics.","src":["jeffdennis","mdopc"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":["cost","retail"],"groups":[]},{"id":"reception-00d2163f4e","kind":"reception","who":"Non-RTO Southeast and regional planners","stance":"negative","text":"The six orders cover organized markets, not the whole country. Southeast voices warn that manufacturers and data centers still need regional transmission planning outside the RTO/ISO frame, and that the regions with weak interconnection practice may be precisely where FERC's show-cause orders do not directly reach.","src":["utilitydive","simonmahan"],"date":"2026-06-29","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"reception-f26055ece0","kind":"reception","who":"FERC (the Commission itself)","stance":"positive","text":"Unanimous action. Chairman Laura V. Swett: ‘We are setting the stage for a resilient, reliable, and forward-thinking grid that empowers communities and safeguards consumers… It also is critical that FERC provide certainty for investors by directing the markets to protect existing deals.’ The orders also invite (don’t foreclose) parallel § 205 filings.","src":["fercPR","fercFS"],"date":"2026-06-18","themes":[],"groups":[]},{"id":"filed-39ca139a9c","kind":"filed","who":"Office of the Ohio Consumers’ Counsel","title":"Ohio wants a seat for consumer advocates","text":"Ohio’s consumer advocate asked FERC to give state consumer offices the same confidential access available to commissions. Customers need an advocate who can test costs and curtailment decisions, it argued. Its November 9 request challenges the October order. It does not ask PJM to publish customer records.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261109-5209","locator":"PDF pp. 14, 18–19","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","flex","retail"]},{"id":"filed-f74a515d01","kind":"filed","who":"Office of the Ohio Consumers’ Counsel","title":"Who can see the customer-level data?","text":"OCC sought rehearing of the October 9 order. It asked FERC to reject the registry unless PJM grants consumer advocates access under confidentiality agreements.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261109-5209","locator":"PDF p. 19","docs":[{"slug":"order-er26-3515-pjm-large-load","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["registry","procedure"]},{"id":"filed-095728843b","kind":"filed","who":"Rep. Frank Pallone Jr. (New Jersey)","title":"Aggregate figures or individual projects?","text":"Pallone wanted as much of the registry made public as possible to improve demand forecasts. FERC records his request separately from the states’ requests for protected access. He spoke as a member of Congress.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=18","locator":"PDF p. 18, ¶39","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition"],"date":null,"themes":["registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-fcf0006ba8","kind":"filed","who":"Pennsylvania Public Utility Commission","title":"Pennsylvania asks to see curtailment data","text":"Pennsylvania’s commission supported creating a registry but wanted state commissions to have access and wanted PJM to record curtailments. Its comments addressed the proposal before FERC rejected interim curtailment service.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260903-5206","locator":"PDF p. 8, section II, Large Load Registry","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-ac2bd31c62","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of PJM States","title":"States need usable records to set retail rules","text":"OPSI wanted meaningful state access to the registry to set retail curtailment rules and allocate costs. Its September comments distinguish the registry, which it supported, from the compensation mechanism it challenged.","url":"https://opsi.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ER26-3515-000-IRAS-Comments.pdf#page=7","locator":"PDF p. 7, section I.D","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","flex","registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-2a0ff87b1b","kind":"filed","who":"Equinix and PJM industrial customers","title":"Give customers a chance to correct errors","text":"Equinix wanted customers notified and allowed to verify or correct the information PJM would use. Industrial customers also requested an appeal and safeguards for commercial data.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=17","locator":"PDF p. 17, ¶38","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["procedure"]},{"id":"filed-7666780453","kind":"filed","who":"Southern Maryland Electric Cooperative","title":"Who answers for a supplier’s data?","text":"The cooperative asked who bears liability when a distributor reports information obtained from another party, how it could recover its compliance costs, and whether the reporting manual would be filed for review.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=16","locator":"PDF pp. 16–17, ¶37","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition"],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-4b16197245","kind":"filed","who":"Independent Market Monitor for PJM","title":"A registry cannot tell an operator what just tripped","text":"The market monitor distinguished enrollment data from real-time telemetry. It said PJM had not shown how it would measure an actual reduction in the proposed interim service.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=18","locator":"PDF p. 18, ¶40","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-7de7102d6a","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David Rosner","title":"Which customers should lose power first?","text":"Rosner wanted voluntary demand response called first, followed by noncritical large loads before homes and other ordinary customers. He objected to keeping demand out of the capacity market forecast. His October concurrence predates the November compliance filing.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=55","locator":"PDF pp. 55–57, Rosner concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"},{"slug":"order-er26-3515-pjm-large-load","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","registry","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-8da26a66e1","kind":"filed","who":"Jefferies analysts, quoted by Utility Dive","title":"Existing generators may gain from the rejection","text":"Jefferies saw the rejection of interim service as favorable to existing independent generators. That was an analyst assessment of market effects, separate from the commission’s reasons for its decision.","url":"https://www.utilitydive.com/news/ferc-pjm-large-load-interconnection-iras-byonc/832716/","locator":"Ethan Howland, October 12, analyst reaction","docs":[{"slug":"order-er26-3515-pjm-large-load","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-fc32a078cf","kind":"filed","who":"Mary Yang, 804 Advisory, interviewed by Latitude Media","title":"A longer wait for private deals","text":"Yang, a former PJM regulatory counsel, expected other priorities to delay a rewrite. She said that uncertainty could slow bilateral arrangements between new loads and generators.","url":"https://www.latitudemedia.com/news/ferc-rejected-pjms-data-center-curtailment-plan-whats-next-for-byoc/","locator":"Maeve Allsup, October 13, interview with Mary Yang","docs":[{"slug":"order-er26-3515-pjm-large-load","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-fc0171411e","kind":"filed","who":"Chairman Laura Swett","title":"Swett wanted a record FERC could test","text":"Swett said the original procurement arrived too late for a deficiency letter or further evidence. She urged a prompt section 205 filing to repair the rejected terms. Her September concurrence did not address the later timetable.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=92","locator":"PDF p. 92, Swett concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"order-er26-3380-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-f59c6cf9a7","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David Rosner","title":"Rosner would charge zones with data center growth","text":"Rosner supported directing costs to zones whose data center growth drove the shortfall. He called on states to set retail tariffs, enforce collateral and decide curtailment priorities. He wanted a return to ordinary hedging and capacity procurement after the backstop.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=93","locator":"PDF p. 93, Rosner concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"order-er26-3380-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","flex","adequacy","retail"]},{"id":"filed-db4759ecb6","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Lindsay See","title":"See wanted the forecast tied to the bill","text":"See wanted dependable generation quickly but said the first proposal left existing customers exposed. Better evidence about which projects will actually connect should guide who pays for the resulting capacity.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=94","locator":"PDF p. 94, See concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"order-er26-3380-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","registry"]},{"id":"filed-8eebdeb6db","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David LaCerte","title":"LaCerte tied speed to ratepayer protection","text":"LaCerte urged PJM to refile promptly rather than wait for more briefs. He said new loads should pay for the capacity they require and cited the commitment to build, bring or buy that capacity.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=96","locator":"PDF pp. 96–97, LaCerte concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"order-er26-3380-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-2279e21db5","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Judy Chang","title":"A promise to pay is only as good as its security","text":"In the separate Duke case, Chang dissented because Duke had not shown that its security requirements backed the full 15-year payment promise. The comparison bears on PJM’s proposed collateral; Chang was not evaluating PJM’s dollar amount.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261009-3054","locator":"Chang dissent, section on security requirements","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"attachment-er27-454-b","group":null}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","duke-nitsa","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["credit"]},{"id":"filed-133a8d7807","kind":"filed","who":"Office of the Ohio Consumers’ Counsel","title":"Ohio wants charges to reach the customers responsible","text":"On rehearing of the September order, OCC argued that households should not pay to cover a shortfall caused by data centers. It separately questioned FERC’s decision to allow sellers to receive their own bid prices.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5263","locator":"PDF pp. 15, 21–23","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["adequacy","procedure"]},{"id":"filed-9dac181437","kind":"filed","who":"Joint Consumer Advocates, including Delaware, D.C., Illinois, Maryland and New Jersey offices","title":"Consumer advocates question a 15-year commitment","text":"The advocates estimated that buying 15 years rather than five could add $13 billion. They also warned that without a location requirement, customers could pay for capacity built far from the shortfall. These were their estimates and objections to the September order.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5262","locator":"PDF pp. 2, 4, 6–7","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-34a4773d47","kind":"filed","who":"Public Service Commission of West Virginia","title":"West Virginia warns restrictive offsets could drive an overbuy","text":"The West Virginia commission warned that complex rules could discourage participants from reporting qualifying capacity. That would make the final purchase larger than necessary.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260821-5145","locator":"PDF pp. 2–4, bilateral matching and offsets","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-37b9bdfa89","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of PJM States","title":"Update the need when a project drops out","text":"OPSI asked PJM to remove withdrawn large projects when setting the procurement target. Otherwise existing customers could pay for generation to meet demand that no longer exists.","url":"https://opsi.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/OPSI-ER26-3380-000-RBP-Comments.pdf#page=3","locator":"PDF pp. 3–4, original August 21 comments","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-23ea4eeb28","kind":"filed","who":"New Jersey Board of Public Utilities","title":"New Jersey wanted demand-side bids to have room to compete","text":"The New Jersey board sought more flexible terms for demand resources and distributed resource aggregations in the original procurement. This concerned auction eligibility; it did not endorse the later peak-shaving formula.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=15","locator":"PDF p. 15, footnote 54","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-ffab01b829","kind":"filed","who":"Illinois Commerce Commission and Joint Consumer Advocates","title":"Fifteen years of bills on one forecast","text":"Illinois regulators and the consumer advocates warned that a single end date for contracts could amplify forecast errors. FERC recorded that concern as it considered freezing load shares at one point in time.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=40","locator":"PDF p. 40, footnote 144","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","registry"]},{"id":"filed-01177ba8f3","kind":"filed","who":"American Municipal Power","title":"AMP distinguishes local load from regional growth","text":"AMP asked FERC to protect systems that did not cause the new demand. It also disputed the suggestion that the forecast table excluded projects PJM had actually counted.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261013-5514","locator":"PDF pp. 7, 15","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-3691f2e609","kind":"filed","who":"Indicated PJM Transmission Owners","title":"The transmission owners dispute who can impose an exit charge","text":"The owners argued that FERC should reject the original exit amendment on cost-causation grounds and that their agreement reserved the relevant filing right to them. Their rehearing request concerned the earlier wording.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5265","locator":"PDF pp. 5–8","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","procedure"]},{"id":"filed-7f2cad74bb","kind":"filed","who":"Constellation Energy Generation","title":"Constellation asks who benefits when an owner leaves","text":"Constellation asked FERC to clarify that it had not assigned the departing owner’s share to suppliers by default. In its view an exiting owner, rather than captive customers, should bear exit costs it causes.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5261","locator":"PDF pp. 16–17","docs":[{"slug":"attachment-er27-454-b","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-a04ade77f1","kind":"filed","who":"FirstEnergy Service Company","title":"FirstEnergy challenged the earlier security requirement","text":"FirstEnergy compared supplier security of $1.5 million per MW under the earlier design with the much smaller security required of sellers. That objection is a benchmark for the revised calculation, not an objection to the new $686,000 figure.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5270","locator":"PDF p. 14","docs":[{"slug":"attachment-er27-454-c","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-658124583d","kind":"filed","who":"FirstEnergy Service Company","title":"Who pays after a generator starts operating?","text":"FirstEnergy said the original shortfall charge stopped once a generator reached commercial operation. It questioned whether a seller then had sufficient incentive to perform throughout its 15-year term.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5270","locator":"PDF p. 13","docs":[{"slug":"attachment-er27-454-b","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-13bc000761","kind":"filed","who":"Amazon and Northern Virginia Electric Cooperative","title":"Should the original forecast bind even if load never arrives?","text":"Amazon argued that immutable shares would diverge from actual demand as projects changed. NOVEC asked why PJM considered a single snapshot appropriate.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=40","locator":"PDF p. 40, responsive pleadings","docs":[{"slug":"attachment-er27-454-d","group":null},{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-fcb4a9a243","kind":"filed","who":"U.S. Department of Energy","title":"DOE wanted individual loads identified before allocation","text":"DOE wanted a mandatory way to identify the large load driving additional procurement and give it a chance to build, bring or buy capacity. It questioned whether the proposed two-year lookback would capture the relevant projects.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261007-5181","locator":"PDF pp. 6–7","docs":[{"slug":"attachment-er27-454-a","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-43d0fa93e5","kind":"filed","who":"Independent Market Monitor for PJM","title":"The market monitor wanted new physical supply","text":"The monitor objected to eligibility rules that could let an existing resource reclassify its capacity. Its test was whether an award actually paid for additional physical supply.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=15","locator":"PDF p. 15, responsive pleadings","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er27-330-pjm-rbp-amendments","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-72f333ddfa","kind":"filed","who":"Northern Virginia Electric Cooperative","title":"NOVEC disputes attributing the whole shortage to large loads","text":"NOVEC identified causes of the capacity shortage besides growth in large loads. It preferred voluntary subscription, with interruptible service for a customer that declined. It is a cooperative, not the Virginia regulator.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261029-5264","locator":"PDF pp. 3–5, 16","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"},{"slug":"filing-er27-330-pjm-rbp-amendments","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["flex"]},{"id":"filed-e9d4cdedaf","kind":"filed","who":"Mississippi and Louisiana Public Service Commissions","title":"Two state commissions back the parallel study","text":"The commissions backed a 120-day joint study. They said studying the load and its generator together could avoid duplicating network upgrades; the limit of ten pairs per period made it manageable. They wanted a permanent process so projects could plan and finance construction.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5073","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":["study","retail"]},{"id":"filed-821b5a376a","kind":"filed","who":"Arkansas Public Service Commission","title":"Arkansas signs on to the Southern regulators’ case","text":"Arkansas expressly agreed with the Mississippi and Louisiana commissions’ comments and asked FERC to accept MISO’s tariff changes. It did not offer its own numerical estimate of costs or queue delays.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5180","locator":"Comments of the Arkansas PSC, concluding paragraph (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study","cost"]},{"id":"filed-bb58def83f","kind":"filed","who":"Iowa Office of Consumer Advocate, Illinois and Michigan attorneys general, Minnesota Department of Commerce and co-protesters","title":"Consumer offices question the treatment of existing load","text":"The joint protesters asked how the study would prevent costs shifting to other customers. They warned that related MISO proposals could exempt about 30 GW of existing load from some reliability requirements; 30 GW was not the size of the proposed LARS study queue.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5197","locator":"PDF pp. 1, 3–4, 6","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","miso-irr","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study","cost","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-eedf95ead6","kind":"filed","who":"Google","title":"Google backs co-study but wants safeguards for independent bids","text":"Google supported studying loads and generators together but questioned the separate assumptions used in the ordinary queue. It wanted cost protections and a way for independent generators to participate without discrimination by the serving utility.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5177","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 6–9","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":["study","cost"]},{"id":"filed-093d4bca07","kind":"filed","who":"Bloom Energy","title":"Bloom proposes 50 MW without delaying the filing","text":"Bloom backed the study but challenged its 200 MW entry threshold. It proposed letting transmission-connected loads of at least 50 MW participate and asked FERC not to delay the requested start date.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5195","locator":"PDF pp. 1, 4–5","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":["study","registry"]},{"id":"filed-f3527b8b64","kind":"filed","who":"American Clean Power Association and Southern Renewable Energy Association","title":"Who gets first claim on spare grid capacity?","text":"The trade associations said studying accelerated projects without other generators already waiting could leave later studies with fewer feasible connections and higher costs. They objected to a permanent parallel route without a cap on total capacity.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5176","locator":"PDF pp. 2, 21, 27","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":["study","cost"]},{"id":"filed-b47253d1ab","kind":"filed","who":"Solar Energy Industries Association and Advanced Energy United","title":"Solar developers challenge a utility veto","text":"The protesters asked FERC to reject the proposal or clarify that an applicant meeting all published requirements could not lose access at the transmission customer’s discretion.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5181","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2, 13","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-dfe3f8365b","kind":"filed","who":"Clean Energy Advocates","title":"A parallel queue can promise the same grid capacity twice","text":"The advocates warned that two study tracks using different assumptions could count the same unused transmission capacity twice. They challenged assigning upgrade costs to existing customers based on agreements whose terms FERC had not seen.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5163","locator":"PDF p. 18","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":["study","cost"]},{"id":"filed-8ec34959ac","kind":"filed","who":"Alliant Energy and Consumers Energy","title":"Utilities say deposits and commitments screen speculative requests","text":"The utilities backed the readiness requirements because a speculative project could occupy one of a limited number of study slots. They argued the joint model could identify cheaper combined upgrades.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5096","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":["study"]},{"id":"filed-e4f238c757","kind":"filed","who":"Entergy and Cleco","title":"Entergy and Cleco contrast 120 days with years in the regular queue","text":"The utilities said some ordinary MISO study cycles had taken four years or more. They supported the 120-day path and argued that its cap of ten load-generator pairs per study period would keep it workable.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5035","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study"]},{"id":"filed-631773db33","kind":"filed","who":"DTE Electric","title":"DTE sees a binding match as evidence of readiness","text":"DTE supported the pairing rules and minimum commitment as evidence of a lasting commercial relationship. It also said the tariff allowed the interconnection customer to participate even though the transmission customer submits the application.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261023-5161","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-e957955d1c","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of MISO States, quoted by Utility Dive","title":"State organization asks for protection of existing queue positions","text":"OMS warned that a rapid route must not turn into a general substitute for the queue. It asked for safeguards before qualifying pairs could claim transmission capacity ahead of projects already waiting.","url":"https://www.utilitydive.com/news/miso-large-load-lars-ferc-google-oms/849887/","locator":"October 26, reporting on October 23 ER27-25 comments","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-25-miso-lars","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study","retail"]},{"id":"filed-14439ec703","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Judy Chang","title":"Chang dissented over the lack of dedicated security","text":"Chang said Duke had not demonstrated that a promise to pay for 75% of forecast demand would protect existing customers if the responsible utility defaulted. She wanted security commensurate with the 15-year commitment.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261009-3054","locator":"Chang dissent, section on security requirements (unpaginated DOCX)","docs":[{"slug":"order-er26-2691-duke-nitsa","group":"duke-nitsa"}],"groups":["duke-nitsa"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-01daddc604","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Lindsay See","title":"See accepts 75% but calls it a low-end safeguard","text":"See saw the minimum revenue commitment as an improvement over charging for use alone. She said 75% might be near the low end of an acceptable range and urged Duke to consider incremental charges where appropriate.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261009-3054","locator":"See concurrence, discussion of committed revenue contribution (unpaginated DOCX)","docs":[{"slug":"order-er26-2691-duke-nitsa","group":"duke-nitsa"}],"groups":["duke-nitsa"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-f554b642c5","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David Rosner","title":"Rosner calls the tariff an improvement, with risk left over","text":"Rosner said Duke’s previous tariff contained no comparable protection for households or small businesses. He accepted the added protection and said FERC should consider more if action by the states proved insufficient.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261009-3054","locator":"Rosner concurrence, first and second points (unpaginated DOCX)","docs":[{"slug":"order-er26-2691-duke-nitsa","group":"duke-nitsa"}],"groups":["duke-nitsa"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-2c1a07c81a","kind":"filed","who":"OPSI and PJM Governors’ Collaborative governance negotiating team","title":"States object to members controlling access to FERC","text":"Before the vote, the states’ negotiating team opposed the members’ term sheet. The team said the proposed supermajority requirement would let private members decide whether a state proposal received equal consideration at FERC. It preferred a direct route for states to be heard.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/-/media/DotCom/about-pjm/who-we-are/public-disclosures/2026/20260930-opsi-and-governors-collaborative-letter-to-the-pjm-board-concerning-the-members-governance-term-sheet.pdf#page=1","locator":"PDF p. 1, September 29 letter","docs":[{"slug":"filing-ad26-7-pjm-governance-vote","group":"pjm-governance"}],"groups":["pjm-governance","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-7b33b41019","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David LaCerte","title":"LaCerte points to gridlock in PJM’s stakeholder process","text":"At WIRES on July 9, LaCerte said PJM’s stakeholder process too often protected individual business interests and prevented agreement. He was discussing governance months before the members voted, not evaluating their final term sheet.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dEisTPju8E0&t=2400s","locator":"July 9 WIRES keynote, 00:40:00","docs":[{"slug":"filing-ad26-7-pjm-governance-vote","group":"pjm-governance"}],"groups":["pjm-governance","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-2e71cef58e","kind":"filed","who":"Chairman Laura Swett","title":"How does a new load forecast change the capacity purchase?","text":"Swett asked the October reliability panel how to model the scale and operating patterns of large loads. She pointed out that the forecast determines how much generation is procured and therefore influences market prices.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e-LMMOKOhcc&t=2735s","locator":"October 21 reliability conference, 00:45:35","docs":[{"slug":"notice-ad26-8-third-supplemental","group":"reliability-conference"}],"groups":["reliability-conference"],"date":null,"themes":["registry","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-5268bf4c45","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Judy Chang","title":"Chang questions the incentive to stay flexible after connection","text":"Chang asked why a data center would voluntarily disconnect once connected if each interrupted megawatt-hour was very costly. She invited utilities and operators to explain what would make a flexibility commitment credible.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e-LMMOKOhcc&t=5783s","locator":"October 21 reliability conference, 01:36:23","docs":[{"slug":"notice-ad26-8-third-supplemental","group":"reliability-conference"}],"groups":["reliability-conference"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","flex"]},{"id":"filed-f946124c49","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David LaCerte","title":"Who pays when a data center brings its own generator?","text":"Answering Senator Cotton, LaCerte said beneficiaries should pay for the new infrastructure and states should hold hyperscalers to their commitments in retail rates. He did not specify a PJM cost formula.","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-119shrg64322/pdf/CHRG-119shrg64322.pdf#page=79","locator":"PDF pp. 79–80, printed pp. 75–76","docs":[{"slug":"e-7-pjm-el26-67-000","group":null},{"slug":"senate-ferc-oversight-20260722","group":"senate-oversight"}],"groups":["senate-oversight","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail"]},{"id":"filed-b4948cbace","kind":"filed","who":"Midcontinent Independent System Operator","title":"MISO proposes parallel studies for paired load and generation","text":"MISO proposed LARS in a separate section 205 docket. It argued that modeling a matched pair could identify upgrades for the combination instead of each project separately. This was one proposed response to the June concerns, not a final decision on the full show cause case.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261002-5074","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 15","docs":[{"slug":"e-8-miso-el26-70-000","group":null}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study"]},{"id":"filed-2d4705a95a","kind":"filed","who":"Morongo Transmission LLC","title":"Tribal transmission owner says it has no end-use customers","text":"Morongo said it leases transmission capacity and receives revenue through CAISO’s access charge. Because it serves no end-use customers, it argued that its own tariff needed no amendment. It still intended to participate in the proceeding.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260812-5158","locator":"PDF pp. 1–3","docs":[{"slug":"e-10-caiso-el26-71-000","group":null}],"groups":["sc-caiso"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-a4261a63dd","kind":"filed","who":"Elliot Mainzer, CAISO president and CEO, quoted by APPA","title":"CAISO describes the board’s plan before filing","text":"After the October board action, Mainzer said CAISO expected its response to improve operational visibility, protect other customers from cost shifts and speed connections for flexible loads. His statement described a plan, not FERC approval.","url":"https://www.publicpower.org/periodical/article/caiso-board-advances-new-procedures-connecting-large-loads","locator":"October 28, comments following board action","docs":[{"slug":"e-10-caiso-el26-71-000","group":null}],"groups":["sc-caiso"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","flex"]},{"id":"filed-d45243939f","kind":"filed","who":"American Municipal Power","title":"AMP wanted an enforceable route back to the show cause case","text":"AMP opposed an unconditional delay and asked FERC to require a compliance filing. The order says FERC would return to the section 206 case for any preliminary finding MISO’s tariff filings did not address.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260814-3058","locator":"Discussion of AMP’s protest and Commission determination (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"abeyance-el26-70-miso","group":"sc-multi"}],"groups":["sc-multi","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-42d0213027","kind":"filed","who":"Southwest Power Pool","title":"Why SPP asked for five more days than other operators","text":"SPP told FERC its board meeting had moved because of the elections. The revised November 17 vote made a November 16 filing impractical; FERC granted a November 20 response date.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260814-3060","locator":"SPP motion and Commission determination (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"abeyance-el26-68-spp","group":"sc-multi"}],"groups":["sc-multi","sc-spp"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-bd4bcae692","kind":"filed","who":"Six Cities and Western Area Power Administration","title":"Six municipal owners and WAPA distinguished themselves from investor-owned utilities","text":"These public owners committed to coordinate with CAISO and file changes if necessary. FERC rescinded the show cause order as to them; the other California respondents remained in the case.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260814-3061","locator":"Commission determination on Six Cities and WAPA (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"abeyance-el26-71-caiso","group":"sc-multi"}],"groups":["sc-multi","sc-caiso"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-dbb2bdd499","kind":"filed","who":"Constellation and PJM","title":"Constellation challenges the July 2028 date","text":"Constellation questioned the proposed July 2028 start for interim network service. PJM answered that administering interruptions required new automation, but said it expected to take requests from September 2027.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261015-5003","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-1479-pjm","group":"pjm-colocation"}],"groups":["pjm-colocation","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-54830e9552","kind":"filed","who":"American Municipal Power and PJM","title":"AMP identifies a missing class of behind-the-meter generation","text":"AMP asked that the new service also cover non-retail behind-the-meter generation. PJM agreed to revise the language. On a separate objection from Amtrak and industrial customers, PJM said its changes did not impose the netting restriction they feared.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261015-5003","locator":"PDF pp. 6–7","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-1479-pjm","group":"pjm-colocation"}],"groups":["pjm-colocation","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["colo","retail"]},{"id":"filed-eba355a4d7","kind":"filed","who":"PJM and Constellation","title":"Which part could be filed in August?","text":"PJM sought an extension to align its co-location and large-load work. Constellation wanted full interim network-service terms on the original schedule. FERC kept the August partial filing while granting additional time for other requirements.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260814-3005","locator":"Requests and ruling on extension of time (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"e-2-pjm-el25-49-002","group":null}],"groups":["pjm-colocation","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["colo","procedure"]},{"id":"filed-2744b13554","kind":"filed","who":"Pennsylvania Office of Consumer Advocate","title":"Pennsylvania’s consumer advocate challenges regional cost sharing","text":"The office asked FERC to clarify that a customer agreement is only one protection. It wanted parties to be able to show that existing regional allocation methods themselves charge other customers for network upgrades needed by a large load.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260717-5246","locator":"PDF pp. 2–5","docs":[{"slug":"e-7-pjm-el26-67-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-dba4609677","kind":"filed","who":"Maryland Office of People’s Counsel, Ohio Consumers’ Counsel, Delaware Division of the Public Advocate and Illinois Attorney General","title":"Four state offices want the full upgrade cost charged to the new load","text":"The four offices backed FERC’s transparency directives but said disclosure alone leaves existing cost shifts intact. They asked that agreements require full payment for the necessary upgrades and that other customers receive the corresponding revenue credits promptly.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260717-5258","locator":"PDF pp. 1–4, section III.B challenge","docs":[{"slug":"e-7-pjm-el26-67-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail"]},{"id":"filed-981b470b62","kind":"filed","who":"Rep. Victoria Spartz (Indiana’s 5th Congressional District)","title":"An Indiana representative asks for federal review of large-load projects","text":"Spartz asked FERC and DOE to scrutinize potentially duplicate load forecasts and to require developers to pay attributable generation and transmission costs. Her November letter represents a member of Congress, not Indiana’s utility commission.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261110-4002","locator":"PDF pp. 1–4","docs":[{"slug":"e-7-pjm-el26-67-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost","registry"]},{"id":"filed-431cb46a3d","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of MISO States","title":"What MISO’s regional report leaves out","text":"OMS said the report does not fully reflect state proceedings on resource planning, retail rates and utility approvals because MISO is not a party to those proceedings. It wanted regional planning to complement, rather than displace, state responsibility for adequacy.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260814-5198","locator":"PDF pp. 1–3","docs":[{"slug":"report-el26-70-miso","group":"sc-miso"}],"groups":["sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-6567a41e48","kind":"filed","who":"Missouri Public Service Commission","title":"Missouri supports five extra days to secure a state review","text":"Missouri backed the 95-day request because SPP’s November 17 board vote fell just beyond the 90-day window. It said the extra days allowed meaningful state input without materially delaying the case.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260807-5150","locator":"PDF pp. 1, 3–5","docs":[{"slug":"motion-el26-68-spp-abeyance","group":"sc-spp"}],"groups":["sc-spp"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-a4bfeb3ffb","kind":"filed","who":"SPP Regional State Committee","title":"SPP’s state committee wanted time to develop a cost proposal","text":"The committee endorsed a November 20 deadline while its cost-allocation team studied who should secure network costs if promised demand fails to arrive or leaves early. The filing endorsed the schedule, not a final cost-allocation formula.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260807-5123","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2","docs":[{"slug":"motion-el26-68-spp-abeyance","group":"sc-spp"}],"groups":["sc-spp"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail","procedure"]},{"id":"filed-4e9132dce5","kind":"filed","who":"New England States Committee on Electricity","title":"New England states back time for regional deliberation","text":"NESCOE, the committee representing the six New England states, supported the requested delay. It wanted the states and stakeholders to be able to examine proposed tariff changes through the region’s existing process.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260807-5196","locator":"PDF pp. 1–4","docs":[{"slug":"motion-el26-72-isone-abeyance","group":"sc-isone"}],"groups":["sc-isone"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-a368d09377","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of MISO States","title":"Support for more time came with a reservation","text":"OMS supported time for a coordinated sequence of filings but said granting abeyance should neither narrow the inquiry nor prejudge that the coming tariff changes would settle every issue.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260807-5186","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 5","docs":[{"slug":"motion-el26-70-miso-abeyance","group":"sc-miso"}],"groups":["sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["retail","procedure"]},{"id":"filed-cc7989c32d","kind":"filed","who":"Corporate Energy Buyers Association","title":"Corporate buyers wanted time to test the California proposal","text":"CEBA backed the California parties’ requests for 90 more days. It argued that testing proposals with stakeholders before filing could improve their commercial viability and legal durability.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260807-5215","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2","docs":[{"slug":"motion-el26-71-caiso-abeyance","group":"sc-caiso"}],"groups":["sc-caiso"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-9a696ceafc","kind":"filed","who":"Constellation Energy Generation","title":"Constellation supported more time with a condition","text":"Constellation supported a partial delay but said PJM’s separate duty to comply with the co-location order could not depend on the outcome of the large-load process.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260803-5234","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3","docs":[{"slug":"motion-el26-67-pjm-abeyance","group":"sc-pjm"}],"groups":["sc-pjm","pjm-colocation"],"date":null,"themes":["colo"]},{"id":"filed-c6fe1b7b78","kind":"filed","who":"Data Center Coalition","title":"Data center operators want the binding standard in the tariff","text":"The coalition asked FERC to reject the reliability proposal without prejudice and require substantive standards in the tariff itself. It said future changes should be filed with FERC rather than made outside the tariff.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261103-5190","locator":"PDF pp. 4–5","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3650-miso-irr","group":"miso-irr"}],"groups":["miso-irr"],"date":null,"themes":["adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-e8e814a36f","kind":"filed","who":"Midwest Transmission Dependent Utilities","title":"Municipal utilities seek a clear limit on regulated loads","text":"The utilities said MISO’s answer disclaimed applying the rules to distribution-connected loads but the tariff still did not say so unambiguously. They wanted the tariff to explain how a utility could assign a compliance task to the large load.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261028-5128","locator":"PDF p. 2","docs":[{"slug":"filing-20261013-5440","group":"miso-irr"}],"groups":["miso-irr","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-5b9a8b0510","kind":"filed","who":"Midcontinent Independent System Operator","title":"MISO says independent generators can negotiate before applying","text":"MISO said the transmission customer submits an application for an already matched pair. It argued that an independent generator remains free to bring a proposal to that customer before the application, and that the eligibility rules are nondiscretionary.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261109-5205","locator":"PDF pp. 20–22","docs":[{"slug":"protest-er27-25-google","group":"miso-lars"},{"slug":"protest-er27-25-seia-united","group":"miso-lars"},{"slug":"protest-er27-25-acp-srea","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-05b313e79c","kind":"filed","who":"Midcontinent Independent System Operator","title":"MISO defends a higher entry threshold","text":"MISO defended 200 MW based on its experience with large-load studies. It said modeling the load and several associated plants together offered the largest potential savings where loads and upgrades were especially large.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261109-5205","locator":"PDF pp. 23–24","docs":[{"slug":"protest-er27-25-bloom","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study"]},{"id":"filed-fa7a8de3ef","kind":"filed","who":"Midcontinent Independent System Operator","title":"MISO would proceed before the rest of the cost protections are approved","text":"MISO said the study should go ahead before related cost-recovery reforms were complete. It promised to incorporate those protections once FERC accepted them and cited other existing tariff mechanisms.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261109-5205","locator":"PDF pp. 5–6","docs":[{"slug":"protest-er27-25-clean-energy-advocates","group":"miso-lars"},{"slug":"protest-er27-25-consumer-advocates","group":"miso-lars"}],"groups":["miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["study","cost"]},{"id":"filed-89c1780396","kind":"filed","who":"Eolian","title":"Eolian wants nearby generation considered for permanent service","text":"Eolian sought clarification that generation located electrically close to a large load could qualify for permanent limited service if it could constrain grid withdrawals over the long term. FERC dismissed the request because the preliminary order was not final.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260921-3056","locator":"Eolian request and FERC discussion (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"e-11-isone-el26-72-000","group":null},{"slug":"e-12-nyiso-el26-69-000","group":null},{"slug":"e-8-miso-el26-70-000","group":null},{"slug":"e-10-caiso-el26-71-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["credit"]},{"id":"filed-9f50cbc567","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Lindsay See","title":"See wants states to retain authority over retail service","text":"See said the federal changes should give states better information about wholesale costs so they could carry out their own affordability duties. She cautioned against using federal tariff reform to override state decisions.","url":"https://www.ferc.gov/media/e-9-el26-68-000","locator":"PDF pp. 77–79, See concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"e-9-spp-el26-68-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail"]},{"id":"filed-3ecd4880e8","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Judy Chang","title":"Chang asks for evidence before narrowing the states’ role","text":"Chang supported developing a regional record. She welcomed evidence from state regulators on possible jurisdictional problems and distinguished the narrower preliminary findings from the broader federal authority contemplated in the national rulemaking.","url":"https://www.ferc.gov/media/e12-el26-69-000","locator":"PDF pp. 109–110, Chang concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"e-12-nyiso-el26-69-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-77a9596860","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David LaCerte","title":"LaCerte urged timely regional answers","text":"LaCerte supported the order as a way to identify gaps in large-load tariffs. He expected a timely response with evidence about the region’s own rules, not a final nationwide tariff imposed in June.","url":"https://www.ferc.gov/media/e11-el26-72-000","locator":"PDF pp. 112–114, LaCerte concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"e-11-isone-el26-72-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-f1a1b52e19","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David Rosner","title":"Rosner wants commitments that survive a canceled project","text":"Rosner argued that a cost recovery agreement should prevent households from paying for a network upgrade if the data center never opens. He invited alternative regional mechanisms if they offered comparable protection.","url":"https://www.ferc.gov/media/e-7-el26-67-000","locator":"PDF p. 91, Rosner concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"e-7-pjm-el26-67-000","group":null}],"groups":[],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-23d93dd1e3","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner Judy Chang","title":"Chang explains why the June record needs upgrade costs","text":"Answering Senator Hirono, Chang linked the June section 206 proceedings to tracking which data center triggered a network upgrade and identifying who would otherwise pay. She described the information needed to limit cost shifting, not the outcome of a regional case.","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-119shrg64322/pdf/CHRG-119shrg64322.pdf#page=75","locator":"PDF p. 75, printed p. 71","docs":[{"slug":"senate-ferc-oversight-20260722","group":"senate-oversight"}],"groups":["senate-oversight"],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-59ea061b6b","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David Rosner","title":"Rosner links investment signals to state confidence","text":"At the July hearing Rosner told Senator McCormick that market rules can send investment signals, but those signals lose force if states do not trust how prices are set. He wanted states involved in regional planning.","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-119shrg64322/pdf/CHRG-119shrg64322.pdf#page=69","locator":"PDF pp. 69–70, printed pp. 65–66","docs":[{"slug":"filing-ad26-7-pjm-governance-vote","group":"pjm-governance"}],"groups":["pjm-governance"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-873a162e2a","kind":"filed","who":"Commissioner David Rosner","title":"When is an interruption a substitute for capacity?","text":"PJM’s July report considered shortage service. Reviewing a subsequent tariff proposal in October, Rosner objected to mandatory curtailment before paid, voluntary demand response and warned about masking demand in capacity prices.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=55","locator":"PDF pp. 55–57, Rosner concurrence","docs":[{"slug":"report-el26-67-pjm","group":"sc-pjm"}],"groups":["sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-cf703fde8a","kind":"filed","who":"Elliot Mainzer, CAISO president and CEO, quoted by APPA","title":"How California described its board-approved plan three months later","text":"The July report scheduled a response for the fall. After the October board action, Mainzer said CAISO expected the proposed rules to improve operational awareness and reduce cost shifts. The board action did not itself change a FERC tariff.","url":"https://www.publicpower.org/periodical/article/caiso-board-advances-new-procedures-connecting-large-loads","locator":"October 28, board action","docs":[{"slug":"report-el26-71-caiso","group":"sc-caiso"}],"groups":["sc-caiso"],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-e00d8318ed","kind":"filed","who":"SPP Regional State Committee","title":"State regulators raised the risk that a large project leaves early","text":"In its later comments on the filing schedule, the committee said its cost team was examining security for upgrades if a large customer never arrives or leaves early. It did not approve a particular conditional-service charge.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260807-5123","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2","docs":[{"slug":"report-el26-68-spp","group":"sc-spp"}],"groups":["sc-spp"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail"]},{"id":"filed-8377f553c9","kind":"filed","who":"Exelon Corporation","title":"Exelon points to New England as a precedent for buying closer to delivery","text":"In a separate complaint about PJM, Exelon argued that moving procurement closer to delivery could reduce forecast risk. It invoked ISO New England as a precedent. The complaint sought changes in PJM, not changes to ISO New England’s July report.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261103-5191","locator":"PDF pp. 52–53","docs":[{"slug":"report-el26-72-isone","group":"sc-isone"}],"groups":["sc-isone","exelon-prompt-auction","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-02ea1c7c87","kind":"filed","who":"New York Independent System Operator","title":"Who decides if a regulated generation solution will go forward?","text":"NYISO explained that if the market and transmission proposals cannot cover a reliability need, it may refer a non-transmission solution to the New York Public Service Commission. The state commission then considers the project and cost recovery. This is NYISO’s description of the process, not a state endorsement of a project.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260720-5217","locator":"PDF pp. 7–8","docs":[{"slug":"e-12-nyiso-el26-69-000","group":null}],"groups":["sc-nyiso"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","adequacy","retail"]},{"id":"filed-8e352da382","kind":"filed","who":"Southwest Power Pool","title":"SPP acknowledges an omitted West Region rate-sheet item","text":"SPP’s November 10 amendment says the November 6 filing inadvertently omitted Schedule 14 and Incremental Market Efficiency Use revenues on the Upper Missouri rate sheet. It corrected that sheet while retaining the conditional-service proposal.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20261110-5175","locator":"PDF p. 2","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er27-432-spp-chills","group":"spp-chills"}],"groups":["spp-chills","sc-spp"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-2a3fe55f91","kind":"filed","who":"Illinois Commerce Commission","title":"Illinois supports a one-time buy, with conditions","text":"The ICC supported a one-time supplemental purchase but wanted protection against forecast error, data for state regulators and no new obligations on states. It warned that a modest percentage error in a larger forecast could mean paying for substantial unneeded capacity.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260819-5134","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2, 4, 6–7","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-3c583d711b","kind":"filed","who":"Public Service Commission of West Virginia","title":"West Virginia prefers to try buyer-seller matching first","text":"The commission opposed the original filing in its proposed form and on its proposed timetable. It wanted PJM to learn how much demand would be covered by bilateral contracts before setting a central procurement target.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260821-5145","locator":"PDF pp. 1–4","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":[]},{"id":"filed-c84fb8d295","kind":"filed","who":"Pennsylvania Office of Consumer Advocate","title":"Pennsylvania’s consumer advocate challenges state-set wholesale obligations","text":"The office argued that PJM was asking states to determine the shares suppliers would owe under a wholesale rate. It asked FERC to reject that design or explain how the new allocation would respect the line between state and federal authority.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260821-5217","locator":"PDF pp. 3–5","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["retail"]},{"id":"filed-bff451253d","kind":"filed","who":"Pennsylvania Office of Consumer Advocate","title":"Who sets a supplier’s share before the bill reaches retail customers?","text":"Pennsylvania’s consumer advocate objected to relying on states to determine wholesale obligations in the original July proposal. The distinction between a wholesale supplier’s share and retail cost recovery remained relevant when PJM refiled.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260821-5217","locator":"PDF pp. 3–5","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail"]},{"id":"filed-3c50d48bc8","kind":"filed","who":"Data Center Coalition","title":"Data centers ask PJM to recognize self-supply","text":"The coalition supported offsetting a new load’s own capacity purchases and procurement through bilateral deals. It also wanted backstop costs charged to loads driving the need rather than customers elsewhere.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260821-5306","locator":"PDF pp. 3–4","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","proximate","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-64ee2df98a","kind":"filed","who":"Data Center Coalition","title":"Corporate buyers oppose a uniform retail cutoff","text":"The coalition supported letting customers bring their own generation but opposed the proposed interim service. It argued that PJM’s region-wide mandatory curtailment rule would displace state retail decisions.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260903-5199","locator":"PDF pp. 2–4","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","retail"]},{"id":"filed-ea544112d7","kind":"filed","who":"Joint Consumer Advocates","title":"Consumer advocates tie curtailment to an alternative to the backstop","text":"The advocates saw value in a choice between contributing new capacity and curtailing with appropriate state action. With suitable eligibility rules, they argued, this mechanism would make a separate backstop procurement redundant.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260903-5212","locator":"PDF pp. 2–3, 40","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","adequacy","retail"]},{"id":"filed-27bf5866d6","kind":"filed","who":"Mississippi and Arkansas Public Service Commissions","title":"Mississippi and Arkansas want binding rules filed with FERC","text":"The two commissions asked FERC to condition acceptance on putting the specific requirements in the tariff. They said MISO could otherwise change costly requirements through business manuals without comparable review. These are not the same signatories as the LARS comments from Mississippi and Louisiana.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260918-5323","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3650-miso-irr","group":"miso-irr"}],"groups":["miso-irr","miso-lars","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-89955bbbdf","kind":"filed","who":"Iowa Office of Consumer Advocate, Illinois and Michigan attorneys general, Minnesota Department of Commerce and co-protesters","title":"Consumer offices challenge the exemption for earlier projects","text":"The coalition challenged MISO’s grandfathering provisions. It said existing loads can also trip or ramp suddenly and that utilities and other customers could inherit the cost of local reliability projects if those loads were exempt.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260918-5333","locator":"Protest and concluding request on proposed section 37A (unpaginated)","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3650-miso-irr","group":"miso-irr"}],"groups":["miso-irr","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","adequacy"]},{"id":"filed-43becbc14c","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of MISO States","title":"State regulators want the export limit to work on the grid as well as on paper","text":"OMS conditionally supported the zero-injection agreement. It wanted enforceable limits on a generator exporting power and asked MISO to explain how paired loads and generators would count in capacity and reliability calculations during outages.","url":"https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260911-5203","locator":"PDF pp. 3–5, 9","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3552-miso-zgia","group":"miso-zgia"}],"groups":["miso-zgia","sc-miso"],"date":null,"themes":["adequacy","retail"]},{"id":"filed-66e41f747e","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of PJM States, recorded member positions","title":"West Virginia declined to join the other commissions on the backstop","text":"OPSI backed a supplemental purchase. Its filing names support from Delaware, D.C., Illinois, Indiana, Kentucky, Maryland, Michigan, New Jersey, North Carolina, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Tennessee and Virginia commissions. The West Virginia commission did not support the comments. OPSI also wanted to recalculate the target when projects withdrew.","url":"https://opsi.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/OPSI-ER26-3380-000-RBP-Comments.pdf#page=1","locator":"PDF pp. 1–4, footnote 2 and comments","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["adequacy","retail"]},{"id":"filed-652f01d3f3","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of PJM States, recorded member positions","title":"Kentucky, Ohio and Virginia abstained on the IRAS comments","text":"Delaware, D.C., Illinois, Indiana, Maryland, Michigan, New Jersey, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Tennessee and West Virginia commissions supported OPSI’s comments. Kentucky, Ohio and Virginia commissions abstained. The vote covered the whole filing: OPSI supported a registry but wanted clarification or separate treatment of curtailment compensation.","url":"https://opsi.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ER26-3515-000-IRAS-Comments.pdf#page=1","locator":"PDF pp. 1, 7–10, footnote 2 and sections I.D–III","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-312db775ff","kind":"filed","who":"Organization of PJM States","title":"States supported the registry while challenging a different payment rule","text":"OPSI supported the registry because states need verified data to allocate retail costs and decide how to curtail load. Its separate objection concerned automatically compensating curtailed projects. Three commissions abstained on the combined comments; that is not a vote against the registry.","url":"https://opsi.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ER26-3515-000-IRAS-Comments.pdf#page=1","locator":"PDF pp. 1, 7–10, footnote 2 and sections I.D–III","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","flex","registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-e00b422214","kind":"filed","who":"New Jersey Board of Public Utilities","title":"New Jersey wanted one cutoff for counting load across zones","text":"FERC records the board’s comparison of one distributor counting projects from 50 MW and another reporting expansions from 20 MW. The board wanted a consistent threshold so that equally sized projects would not pay differently just because of where they connected.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=43","locator":"PDF pp. 43–44, NJBPU comments summarized by FERC","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-089761bbd9","kind":"filed","who":"New Jersey Board of Public Utilities","title":"New Jersey had objected to uneven starting data","text":"In comments on the July proposal, the board showed how different distributor cutoffs could charge comparable projects differently. It sought a uniform threshold before assigning bills. That objection explains why it matters which projects count in the refiling.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=43","locator":"PDF pp. 43–44, NJBPU comments summarized by FERC","docs":[{"slug":"refiling-er27-454-pjm-backstop","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["cost"]},{"id":"filed-c25bc0c03b","kind":"filed","who":"Office of the Federal Energy Advocate, Public Utilities Commission of Ohio","title":"Ohio’s federal energy advocate favored a state role in retail allocation","text":"FERC records support from the PUCO office for allowing states to determine the allocation to large loads. This is a different office and a different issue from the Ohio Consumers’ Counsel request for confidential registry access.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=47","locator":"PDF p. 47, responsive pleadings, citing Ohio FEA comments p. 4","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop","pjm-large-load-definition"],"date":null,"themes":["registry","retail"]},{"id":"filed-fa03a3db1a","kind":"filed","who":"Google","title":"Google asked FERC to separate the registry from mandatory interim service","text":"FERC records Google’s request to accept the registry on its own. Google argued that getting better large-load data need not depend on approval of PJM’s wider curtailment and compensation design.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=16","locator":"PDF p. 16, ¶36, citing Google protest pp. 3, 18","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3515-pjm-iras","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["flex","registry"]},{"id":"filed-53e6578404","kind":"filed","who":"Google","title":"A separate registry was one remedy Google had sought","text":"In the original proceeding Google argued that FERC could accept the registry without accepting the remaining interim-service terms. This predates PJM’s compliance filing implementing that separation.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9207/20261009-er26-3515-000.pdf#page=16","locator":"PDF p. 16, ¶36, citing Google protest pp. 3, 18","docs":[{"slug":"compliance-er26-3515-pjm","group":"pjm-large-load-definition"}],"groups":["pjm-large-load-definition","sc-pjm"],"date":null,"themes":["registry"]},{"id":"filed-267c6aff3d","kind":"filed","who":"Google, Equinix and Data Center Coalition","title":"Google and other buyers ask for state access to cost-allocation data","text":"FERC cites all three commenters as seeking to share the underlying allocation data with states. Those data would let a retail regulator see which wholesale obligations had been assigned before setting the customer’s retail rate.","url":"https://www.pjm.com/pjmfiles/directory/etariff/FercOrders/9154/20260929-er26-3380-000.pdf#page=49","locator":"PDF p. 49, footnote 191, citing Google comments p. 8","docs":[{"slug":"filing-er26-3380-pjm-original","group":"pjm-backstop"}],"groups":["pjm-backstop"],"date":null,"themes":["cost","retail"]}],"discourseLabels":{"groups":{"sc-pjm":"PJM (E-7)","sc-miso":"MISO (E-8)","sc-spp":"SPP (E-9)","sc-caiso":"CAISO (E-10)","sc-isone":"ISO-NE (E-11)","sc-nyiso":"NYISO (E-12)","sc-multi":"Several dockets","pjm-colocation":"PJM co-location","pjm-large-load-definition":"PJM Large Load registry","pjm-backstop":"PJM backstop auction","pjm-backstop-rehearing":"PJM auction: rehearing","exelon-prompt-auction":"Exelon complaint","miso-lars":"MISO LARS","miso-zgia":"MISO zero-injection GIA","miso-irr":"MISO load reliability rules","spp-chills":"SPP CHILLS","duke-nitsa":"Duke large-load charges","reliability-conference":"Reliability conference","pjm-governance":"PJM governance vote","pjm-stakeholder":"PJM stakeholder record","senate-oversight":"Senate oversight hearing"},"themes":{"study":"Study process and queue","cost":"Who pays for upgrades","colo":"Co-location and on-site generation","flex":"Flexible and curtailable service","proximate":"Generation serving nearby load","registry":"Load visibility and forecasts","adequacy":"Capacity and resource adequacy","retail":"State and federal authority","credit":"Collateral and exit terms","procedure":"Schedule and procedure"}}});
